window.MANUSCRIPT_VERSIONS = [{"v": 0, "date": "2026-09-22 10:46", "note": "original manuscript2_html", "html": "\n<h1>Online estimation of the NCF stability indicator</h1>\n<p>This section derives the normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) for the outer controller loop. The estimator combines three branches: direct identification from displacement measurements, residual output-power inference, and a healthy-model prior. The following formulation develops these branches and combines their contributions into a single indicator for adaptive control.</p><h2>1. Outer controller loop and NCF margin</h2><p>Figure 1 shows the outer controller loop, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the desired displacement obtained from the numerical substructure after phase-lead compensation. The signal <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the command sent by the controller to the transfer system, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. The controller is denoted by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> is the Laplace variable. The plant <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> represents the transfer system and physical substructure. It includes the actuator and servo-valve dynamics, together with control–structure interaction. The parameter <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> denotes the specimen stiffness.</p><p>The adaptive factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math> scales the controller output. The adaptive controller automatically updates <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math> based on the online stability indicator to maintain stable operation and tracking performance. The open-loop dynamics are given by</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div><p>The plant relates the control command to the measured displacement as</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div><p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, respectively. Hence, the closed-loop response is</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div><p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the Laplace transform of the desired displacement.</p><figure id=\"fig1\"><svg aria-labelledby=\"loop-title loop-desc\" role=\"img\" viewbox=\"0 0 1000 280\">\n<title id=\"loop-title\">Outer controller loop for NCF OSI estimation</title>\n<desc id=\"loop-desc\">Desired displacement u enters the summing junction. The controller C produces an output scaled by adaptive factor lambda. The resulting command v drives the transfer system and physical substructure H. Measured actuator displacement y returns to the summing junction.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"loop-arrow\" markerheight=\"7\" markerwidth=\"7\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\" refx=\"9\" refy=\"5\" viewbox=\"0 0 10 10\"><path d=\"M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 Z\" fill=\"#333\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#333\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<path d=\"M 28 108 H 124\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><circle cx=\"148\" cy=\"108\" r=\"23\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M 171 108 H 226\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n<rect height=\"78\" width=\"153\" x=\"228\" y=\"69\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 381 108 H 426\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><rect height=\"78\" width=\"130\" x=\"428\" y=\"69\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 558 108 H 626\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path><rect height=\"118\" width=\"250\" x=\"628\" y=\"49\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M 878 108 H 979\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M 915 108 V 224 H 148 V 132\" marker-end=\"url(#loop-arrow)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"915\" cy=\"108\" fill=\"#333\" r=\"3.5\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\"><text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"71\" y=\"88\">u(t)</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"104\">+</text><text x=\"148\" y=\"122\">−</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"304\" y=\"101\">C(s)</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"304\" y=\"126\">Controller</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"493\" y=\"101\">λ</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"493\" y=\"126\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"587\" y=\"88\">v(t)</text><text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"753\" y=\"83\">H(s, k)</text>\n<text x=\"753\" y=\"114\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"753\" y=\"142\">and physical substructure</text>\n<text font-style=\"italic\" x=\"946\" y=\"88\">y(t)</text><text class=\"note\" x=\"532\" y=\"251\">Measured actuator displacement</text></g></svg>\n<figcaption>Figure 1. Outer controller loop. The adaptive factor scales the controller output before the command is sent to the transfer system and physical substructure.</figcaption></figure><p>The NCF margin measures robustness to perturbations in a normalized coprime factor description <a href=\"#citation1\">[1]</a>. For an internally stable interconnection of a general plant <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and controller <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the margin is defined by</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">‖</mo><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>[</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∞</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div><p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> is the identity matrix and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mo>·</mo><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">‖</mo><mo>∞</mo></msub></mrow></math> is the <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mo>∞</mo></msub></mrow></math> norm, defined as the largest singular value maximized over frequency. Following the SISO derivation presented by Gomaa et al. <a href=\"#citation2\">[2]</a>, this expression becomes</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div><p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> is angular frequency and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is the imaginary unit. This form avoids a numerical singular-value calculation. For the present indicator, the forward path is grouped into <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> with <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The resulting margin is</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></msub><mi>δ</mi><mo fence=\"true\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" minsize=\"1.2em\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" maxsize=\"1.2em\" minsize=\"1.2em\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div><p>At a given angular frequency, the substitution <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> gives the complex open-loop response <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. This value contains both the magnitude and phase of the loop response. The function <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the normalized chordal distance between this response and the critical point <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div><p>As the open-loop response approaches <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the numerator approaches zero. Consequently, a smaller value of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> indicates a smaller stability margin. The three estimator branches apply the following common mapping to their respective loop-response estimates:</p><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div><h2>2. Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, using the latest <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of displacement data. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the update and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index the samples within that window. The number of samples is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency. Denote the current segments by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Their windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> is the frequency-bin index and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The Hann window is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math>. Windowing reduces the influence of discontinuities at the segment boundaries. Averaging windowed spectral estimates follows the modified-periodogram approach introduced by Welch <a href=\"#citation3\">[3]</a>.</p>\n<p>At each frequency, the instantaneous input power and output power are accompanied by the cross-power term:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The superscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></math> denotes complex conjugation. These are unnormalized spectral powers. The common normalization cancels in the ratios used below. To reduce fluctuations between successive windows, each term is updated through an exponentially weighted average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>An overbar denotes the averaged estimate. The smoothing coefficient is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.39</mn></mrow></math>, and the first complete window initializes each estimate. Exponential averaging assigns progressively smaller weights to older observations <a href=\"#citation4\">[4]</a>. It therefore provides a short memory while allowing the spectra to follow changes in the experiment.</p>\n<p>For <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains 1024 samples and the bin spacing is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The retained frequency set is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>Excluding the line-noise interval leaves 47 frequency bins. In the following equations, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math> denotes a frequency at which the estimator is evaluated. Spectra are written as functions of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math>, while transfer-function values are written at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi></mrow></math>. The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> identifies the current update and its operating condition <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the current, generally unknown stiffness. Thus, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> estimates the current response <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>; no separate estimate of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is required.</p>\n<h2>3. Confidence-weighted identification</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give a measured estimate of the closed-loop response and its magnitude-squared coherence:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The hat denotes an estimate. Coherence lies between zero and one and measures the consistency of the linear command–response relationship. Interpreting the spectral ratio as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with the command. This assumption requires care in RTHS because the command may itself be influenced by feedback through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore supporting evidence, rather than proof of an unbiased estimate; feedback-related identification bias is discussed by Forssell and Ljung <a href=\"#citation5\">[5]</a>.</p>\n<p>The confidence assigned to this estimate also depends on command activity. Define the root-mean-square command amplitude <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and a binary activity variable <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>The activity threshold <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> has the same displacement units as <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Its selected value is 0.0575 in the recorded displacement units, approximately <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation estimated from a quiet record.</p>\n<p>Within an active window, a frequency bin may still contain little command energy. Its relative input power <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and input leverage <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are defined as</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">B</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz analysis grid, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>ν</mi></mrow></math> is a frequency on that grid, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is a dimensionless reference level. The leverage suppresses the influence of bins with weak input power. Combining these measures gives the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>For a bin with vanishing input power, the measured ratio is not used and its confidence is taken as zero. The confidence is a weighting factor, not a statistical confidence probability.</p>\n<p>When measured information is limited, the estimate is drawn toward the healthy loop model. Let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> denote the healthy full-gain loop, where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the initial specimen stiffness. At update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the adaptive factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> gives the prior responses</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></math> denotes the model prior. In particular, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the healthy-model response at the current adaptive factor. The usable closed-loop estimate is the confidence-weighted combination</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>The tilde denotes the blended response. The reconstructed open-loop estimate follows by solving Equation (3) for <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. Thus, a well-supported measurement governs the estimate, while a weakly supported measurement leaves it close to the prior.</p>\n<p>The identified margin curve <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is then obtained from Equation (8). The corresponding prior curve is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mover><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>The operator <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a moving median over three adjacent frequency bins. It reduces isolated spectral irregularities. Both curves remain functions of frequency until the evidence is combined.</p>\n<h2>4. Residual output-power inference</h2>\n<p>Weak command excitation limits direct identification, but the measured response can retain evidence of a change in the loop. To establish a reference, the first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent healthy operation at full gain. Their median output power is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>median</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The index <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> labels the reference windows, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is the windowed transform of the measured displacement in reference window <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. The fixed reference power is denoted by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The observed ratio <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> measures subsequent changes relative to it.</p>\n<p>When the desired displacement contributes little to the measured response, an equivalent additive output disturbance is filtered by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. If the disturbance spectrum remains approximately unchanged from the healthy reference, a candidate loop <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> predicts the output-power ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>The positive, dimensionless factor <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> represents an effective change in loop magnitude. It is not a direct estimate of specimen stiffness. Equation (32) also shows the assumption behind the inference: changes in disturbance intensity or command-driven response can produce output-power changes that are unrelated to stability.</p>\n<p>At each retained frequency, a candidate is selected by matching the observed and modeled power ratios in logarithmic form:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo></mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Here, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> contains 25 candidate values between 0.5 and 6, and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> denotes the natural logarithm. Logarithmic comparison measures relative discrepancies between positive power ratios. The fit is performed independently at each frequency, so the resulting <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> curve is an inference model rather than a uniquely identified physical transfer function.</p>\n<p>The fitted change is retained through a logarithmic memory. Denote the remembered value by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, initialized at one, and let <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> be the target of the current update. The remembered value is updated as an exponentially weighted average in logarithmic space:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>During accumulation of residual evidence, the target is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the update coefficient is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow></math>. Recovery is assessed using the band-median observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>median</mo><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>When <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the recovery target is one and <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.27</mn></mrow></math>. The remembered change then decays toward its healthy value. In the absence of a fitting or recovery update, it is held. This memory allows previously detected changes to remain represented during periods of weak excitation.</p>\n<p>The residual margin curve is evaluated using the same distance as the identified branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>The subscript <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></math> denotes residual output-power inference. Using a common distance permits the two branches to be combined at the same frequency.</p>\n<h2>5. Evidence fusion and reported NCF OSI</h2>\n<p>The contribution of the residual branch depends on the size of the inferred change. Define its detection weight <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>The selected detection scale is <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.75</mn></mrow></math>. The clipping operator limits its argument to the stated interval. The weight increases continuously from zero at <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>g</mi><mo accent=\"true\">―</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at the selected detection scale <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. This scale determines how strongly an inferred loop change contributes to the indicator.</p>\n<p>The confidence <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> assigns weight to direct identification. The residual branch receives part of the remaining weight, with any unsupported share assigned to the prior:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div><div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>These three nonnegative weights sum to one. At each retained frequency, the combined distance estimate is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq41\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtable columnalign=\"left\" rowspacing=\"0.65em\"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(41)</span></div>\n<p>All terms on the right-hand side are evaluated at the same frequency. The symbol <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> distinguishes this combined curve from the final scalar indicator. When command activity vanishes, <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, but remembered residual evidence may still contribute. If neither branch supplies evidence of a change, the estimate returns to the current gain-scaled prior. Such a value remains model-supported rather than newly measured.</p>\n<p>Frequency reduction is performed after fusion. Sort the 47 retained values of <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> in ascending order and denote the second-smallest value by <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The instantaneous indicator is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq42\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(42)</span></div>\n<p>where <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the selected frequency. This reduction discards one lowest value so that a single low bin does not govern the complete estimate. It is a deliberate robustness choice in the estimator, and it can also suppress a genuine narrow frequency minimum.</p>\n<p>Finally, the reported NCF OSI is obtained by smoothing the instantaneous value in time:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq43\"><div class=\"expression\"><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(43)</span></div>\n<p>The coefficient <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math> specifies the fraction of the newest estimate admitted at each update. Temporal smoothing reduces fluctuations, but introduces a delay in following abrupt changes.</p>\n<p>The resulting NCF OSI tracks changes in the stability margin within the monitored frequency band. Its interpretation depends on the available excitation and the healthy-model prior. Residual inference also depends on the assumed disturbance spectrum. Frequency filtering and the removal of one lowest value mean that the reported indicator is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency NCF margin. The adaptive controller uses this indicator to update <math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> during the experiment.</p>\n<section aria-labelledby=\"references-title\" class=\"references\">\n<h2 id=\"references-title\">References</h2>\n<ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H∞-bounded uncertainty,” <em>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</em>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a>.</li><li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An Online Stability Indicator for Real-Time Hybrid Simulation Emphasizing Physical Substructure Degradation,” author manuscript, Section 4.</li><li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <em>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</em>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a>.</li><li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <em>Technometrics</em>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a>.</li><li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <em>Automatica</em>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a>.</li></ol>\n</section>\n"}, {"v": 1, "date": "2026-09-23 14:26", "note": "journal redesign with review edits", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) introduced in the authors’ previous study [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the actuator control loop in hybrid simulation to degradation of the specimen. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the test from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. Three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, of the closed loop from the measured signals. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which detects a change in the loop from the change in measured output power. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the test. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the test from the estimated indicator. The derivation of the SISO margin and its relation to specimen damage are given in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] and are restated here only as required by the estimator.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the closed-loop estimate and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 2, "date": "2026-09-23 15:20", "note": "applied v1 review comments 1-4", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 3, "date": "2026-09-23 17:16", "note": "edited in the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections. hi</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 4, "date": "2026-09-23 17:17", "note": "edited in the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 5, "date": "2026-09-23 17:42", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.<span data-user-edit=\"\"> hi</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 6, "date": "2026-09-23 17:46", "note": "Recovery copy before reverting to v2", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. That study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification rather than a time delay. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. This section describes how the indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement. To keep the indicator reliable when the excitation is weak or changing, three sources of evidence are combined at each frequency. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response function of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. When the loop changes at a given frequency, this branch detects that change by comparing the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, obtained before the experiment. Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 7, "date": "2026-09-23 19:31", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. <span data-user-edit=\"\">The</span> study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification <span data-user-edit=\"\">mechanism.</span> A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. <span data-user-edit=\"\">In</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">this section we extend the analytical study to be implementd experimentally and discuss the experimental challenges and propose how to address them. T</span><span data-user-edit=\"\">he</span> indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator <span data-user-edit=\"\">displacement</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">combining</span> the <span data-user-edit=\"\">values</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">of</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">three</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">estimator</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">branches</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">at</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">each</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">frequency</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">weighted</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">our</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">confidence</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">in</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">them.</span> The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop <span data-user-edit=\"\">traansfer</span> of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <span data-user-edit=\"\">bpow,</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">which</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">can</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">detect</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">the</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">transfer</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">function</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">amplifications</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">at</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">frequencies</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">that</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">cannot</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">be</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">reliably</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">identified.&nbsp; bpow</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">compares</span> the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation.<span data-user-edit=\"\">&nbsp;</span>The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, <span data-user-edit=\"\">which</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">cn</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">be</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">used</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">s&nbsp; fallback when no sufficeient information re vailable. </span>Moreover, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, the controller output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, which is updated during the experiment from the estimated indicator. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.<span data-user-edit=\"\"> hi</span></p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 8, "date": "2026-09-23 20:07", "note": "applied v6 comments on user v7 (kept user's extension sentence, framework as estimator + adaptive controller)", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. A controller tuned to overcome the initial stiffness of the specimen produces a larger displacement response once the specimen loses stiffness [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. In this section, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The resulting framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that uses it. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator. It scales the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in the following sections.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 9, "date": "2026-09-23 20:34", "note": "Saved from the review page", "author": "user", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] <span data-user-edit=\"\">was</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">introduced to quantify </span>the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this section, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The <span data-user-edit=\"\">proposed</span> framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that <span data-user-edit=\"\">gets</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">updated</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">accordingly. </span>The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated <span data-user-edit=\"\">indicator</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">by</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">scaling</span> the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in <span data-user-edit=\"\">this</span> <span data-user-edit=\"\">section.</span></p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the hybrid simulation loop with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is then passed through the phase-lead compensator to obtain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> forms a command that is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, and therefore includes control–structure interaction. The measured actuator displacement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is returned to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"></rect><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"></circle>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"></path>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is evaluated with the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, for which the general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>δ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All branches of the estimator evaluate the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, that is, where the loop response lies far from the critical point. The cap removes credit from these frequencies and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The estimator is updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and the bin spacing is 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. The instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>are unnormalized, because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below. The argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. Following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], each spectrum is smoothed recursively and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis set retains the 2–100 Hz band and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∉</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&nbsp;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The ratio is an unbiased estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. This is not guaranteed in hybrid simulation, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>The weight given to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> combines three factors. Command activity is detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. Within an active window, bins that carry little command energy are discounted through the input leverage</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ν</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ℬ</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement is replaced by the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed, and the loop is recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>A well-supported measurement therefore governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The identified and prior margin curves are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>When the command is weak, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unreliable, but the measured output power still reflects the loop. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain and define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period, the output is shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>The positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">|</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. The fitted change is retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>Ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference, and the memory decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. The output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The three branches are weighted at each frequency by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>which are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement, the output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. The idle case needs no special treatment: when the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin, an unchanged loop reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mrow><mo>*</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. The reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and is used by the adaptive law to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one update, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"></path></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"></rect><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"></rect>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"></rect>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"></path><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"></circle>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>τ</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Ω</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>𝒢</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">^</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real, so the output-power branch represents changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and it follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 10, "date": "2026-09-23 23:27", "note": "rewrote 2.1-2.6 for flow (reason first, known-to-new), kept user's v9 + working-copy lead", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>Before the estimator can be formulated, the loop whose stability it monitors must be defined. Figure 1 shows this loop for a hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. The phase-lead compensator then advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to offset the lag of the transfer system, which gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so it includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> closes the hybrid loop by returning to the numerical substructure.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The estimator monitors the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, because specimen degradation acts directly on this loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To judge how close this loop is to instability, the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is used, as in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, its general definition reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The cap is needed because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, so the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. These frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. Capping them removes this spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency, which must be obtained from measured signals while the experiment runs. For this purpose, the estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>Not every bin is useful. The analysis set keeps the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator and rejects line noise:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated. The three branches that follow all start from these averaged spectra.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The most direct way to obtain the loop response is to identify it from the reference and the measured response. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because the estimate can be poor, the estimator decides how much to trust it in each bin. This confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the measurement must be replaced by something more reliable, and the estimator uses the initial-state prior at the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. Using it requires a reference. The first three non-overlapping windows are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain, and they define the initial reference power and the observed ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, suppose that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance whose spectrum is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and a detected change must persist once the excitation fades. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory must also forget a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is judged from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>Each frequency now has three margin estimates, and the estimator must decide how much each one counts. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>The adaptive controller needs a single number, so the fused curve is reduced over frequency. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 11, "date": "2026-09-23 23:36", "note": "revised 2.1-2.6 for the reader: no writer notes, no whose, phase lead framed by its purpose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>The indicator monitors the stability of the hybrid simulation loop shown in Figure 1. In this loop, the numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The plant therefore includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the hybrid loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Within this hybrid loop, specimen degradation acts directly on the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the general definition of the margin reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>During the experiment, the loop response needed in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is unknown and is estimated from the measured signals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The estimator analyses short windows of these signals and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 12, "date": "2026-09-23 23:43", "note": "fixed technical errors found against Paper 1 (outer controller loop, no 'hybrid loop', degradation changes loop dynamics)", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>To show where the indicator operates, Figure 1 presents the hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which comprises the transfer system (servo-valve and actuator) and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The plant therefore includes control–structure interaction. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. For the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the general definition of the margin reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 13, "date": "2026-09-23 23:48", "note": "transfer system includes inner-loop controller and CSI, margin lumped in L stated as a difference from [2]", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>To show where the indicator operates, Figure 1 presents the hybrid simulation with the adaptive controller. The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground motion and the measured force. A phase-lead compensator, designed to improve the tracking of the transfer system, advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and gives the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and applied as the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, the servo-valve, the actuator, and the control–structure interaction between the actuator and the specimen. Finally, the measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 14, "date": "2026-09-24 10:49", "note": "user's rewrite of the first 2.1 paragraphs", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then acts on the estimated indicator by scaling the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, updated during the experiment, to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To offset the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> applied to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. Control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> returns to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with adaptive controller setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 15, "date": "2026-09-24 11:16", "note": "ported the user's unsaved v14 edits exactly", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilize the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. Whil the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 16, "date": "2026-09-24 11:16", "note": "fixed three slips in the user's edits: utilizes, compensate for, while", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the dynamics of the outer controller loop, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, change [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore evaluates this loop. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, its open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As in [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], the distance of this loop from instability is measured by the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]. Unlike [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], which separates the outer controller from the plant, the whole loop is lumped here into the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The general definition of the margin then reduces to</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> at any frequency therefore signals a loop close to instability. All branches of the estimator evaluate a capped form of this distance,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Such frequencies lie far from the critical point and say nothing about the margin. The cap removes their spurious credit and bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 17, "date": "2026-09-24 11:29", "note": "Clarify loop margin and capped distance after Figure 1; preserve equations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Specimen degradation reduces stiffness and changes the dynamics of the outer controller loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore monitors the stability margin of this loop from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] quantifies the robustness of the loop to perturbations. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] evaluated this margin by treating the outer controller and the plant separately. Here the complete loop transfer function is assigned to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This formulation allows the margin to be evaluated directly from the loop response. The NCF margin for this formulation is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at each frequency. The smallest distance over all frequencies determines the margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> therefore indicates proximity to instability. To limit how much larger distances contribute to the reported indicator, all branches of the estimator use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits the contribution from these frequencies while preserving smaller distances that indicate proximity to the critical point. It therefore bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> without altering those smaller distances.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 18, "date": "2026-09-24 11:30", "note": "Remove repetition from capped-distance explanation; preserve equations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Specimen degradation reduces stiffness and changes the dynamics of the outer controller loop [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>]. The indicator therefore monitors the stability margin of this loop from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the open-loop and closed-loop transfer functions are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] quantifies the robustness of the loop to perturbations. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] evaluated this margin by treating the outer controller and the plant separately. Here the complete loop transfer function is assigned to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This formulation allows the margin to be evaluated directly from the loop response. The NCF margin for this formulation is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>The function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at each frequency. The smallest distance over all frequencies determines the margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. A small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> therefore indicates proximity to instability. To limit how much larger distances contribute to the reported indicator, all branches of the estimator use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits the contribution from these frequencies while preserving smaller distances that indicate proximity to the critical point. It therefore bounds the reported indicator by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 19, "date": "2026-09-24 12:21", "note": "Tighten prose after Figure 1 and remove repeated loop rationale", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>For the outer controller loop in Figure 1, define the plant transfer function as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] treated the outer controller and plant separately. For this calculation, we assign the complete loop to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and use unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to evaluate the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] from the loop response:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> measures the chordal distance from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is the smallest such distance over all frequencies. To limit the contribution of larger distances, all estimator branches use the capped distance</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that the distance exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits these larger values while preserving smaller distances to the critical point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 20, "date": "2026-09-24 12:43", "note": "Restore connected impersonal prose after Figure 1", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, respectively. For the NCF calculation, Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] represented the outer controller and plant separately. In the present formulation, the complete outer controller loop is represented as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with unit feedback <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] can therefore be evaluated from the loop response:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>In <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>, the frequency infimum of the chordal distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> identifies the closest approach of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the critical point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. A smaller margin therefore indicates greater proximity to instability. Because large distances do not signal proximity to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, they are capped in every estimator branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>The identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> shows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> exceeds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> only where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The cap limits these larger distances while preserving smaller distances to the critical point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the loop response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be obtained from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the full-gain loop identified at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. Applying the capped distance to both loops gives the identified and prior margin curves</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the same capped distance as <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003D5;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (6)–(8)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (10)–(16)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (17)–(22)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">λL<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (14), (16)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (23)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Eqs. (26)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Eq. (24) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, <a href=\"#eq8\">(8)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, <a href=\"#eq12\">(12)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 21, "date": "2026-09-24 13:03", "note": "Use controller–plant NCF margin and remove capped distance", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the adaptive factor included in the outer controller, the NCF margin [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>] is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> requires the plant response at each frequency. Because this response changes as the specimen degrades, it must be inferred from the measured signals while the experiment runs. The estimator analyses short windows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and updates its estimate every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math>. Each update uses the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of data, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window holds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, which gives a bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the samples in the window, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. From these transforms, the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>These spectra are left unnormalized because the normalization cancels in every ratio used below, and the argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. A single window is too noisy to use directly. Each spectrum is therefore smoothed recursively, following the averaging of modified periodograms [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], and initialized by the first complete window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The analysis is limited to the 2–100 Hz band monitored by the indicator, and the bins affected by line noise are excluded:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>which leaves 47 bins. Hereafter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, and the subscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> denotes the operating condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The current stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 22, "date": "2026-09-24 13:10", "note": "Address equation comments and revise spectral-estimation prose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The plant response changes as the specimen degrades, so the margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> is estimated from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> during the experiment. The estimator updates every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of these signals. Consecutive windows therefore overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples, giving a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The resulting instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce single-window variability, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator monitors the 2–100 Hz band and excludes frequencies around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 23, "date": "2026-09-24 13:37", "note": "Introduce short-time spectral estimation before its implementation details", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Short-time spectral estimation forms auto- and cross-spectra from recent records of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To keep the spectral estimates responsive to evolving outer controller loop dynamics as the specimen degrades, each update uses a short recent record. These spectra support the subsequent identification of the outer controller loop response and estimation of the margin in Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>. This subsection defines the windowed transforms, recursive averaging, and frequency bins used to form them.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous auto- and cross-spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 24, "date": "2026-09-24 19:56", "note": "Explain the need for spectral estimation and remove suspended hyphens", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI) demonstrated by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] was introduced to quantify the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that stiffness degradation of the specimen can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical study to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise in the experiment, and propose how to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts, an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop, from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect amplification of the loop at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when no sufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then utilizes the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to keep the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. The evaluation of the framework is discussed in detail in this section.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for the transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to produce the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. Its output is scaled by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> provides feedback to the outer controller and closes the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 25, "date": "2026-09-24 20:13", "note": "Replace framework overview and control-loop paragraphs with author revisions", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The normalized coprime factor online stability indicator (NCF OSI), introduced by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its evidence. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop. In contrast, the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> feeds back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 26, "date": "2026-09-24 21:39", "note": "Port unsaved author edits from review page", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 27, "date": "2026-09-24 21:44", "note": "Improve framework overview and control-loop prose with current writing guide", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">This study extends the NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI) proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>] to experimental implementation. The indicator quantifies closed-loop robustness as the physical plant changes during the experiment. Their analysis showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. The experimental framework combines an online estimator with an adaptive controller.</p>\n<p>The estimator forms three margin curves at each frequency. Direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, uses the controller reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. It derives a margin from the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimate [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] of the closed-loop transfer function from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, can detect loop amplification where direct identification is unreliable by comparing measured output power with a pre-excitation reference. The prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, supplies a fallback when information from measured signals is insufficient. The branches are weighted by confidence in their output before their margins are combined. The adaptive controller uses the resulting indicator to set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which scales the outer controller output. This adjustment seeks an adequate stability margin while retaining as much tracking performance as possible during specimen degradation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>The numerical substructure in Figure 1 computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A phase-lead compensator advances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to offset transfer-system lag and improve tracking. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> responds to the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is measured actuator displacement. The adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> scales the controller output to form the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant combines the transfer system with the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. Control–structure interaction couples the actuator and specimen and is included in the transfer-system dynamics. Force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to close the numerical substructure loop, while displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to close the outer controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Spectral identification of the outer controller loop's closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight applied to the response estimate at each frequency. As the specimen changes during the experiment, these spectra are formed from short recent records to help the estimates reflect the current operating condition.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> using the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of both signals, so consecutive windows overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> indexing the update and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexing its samples, the Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 28, "date": "2026-09-24 21:47", "note": "Refine Section 2.2 spectral estimation opening and restore surrounding prose", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. Short recent records help these spectra reflect changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>The spectra are updated every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.125</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> from the most recent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00073;</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Consecutive windows therefore overlap by 75%. At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow></math> samples and has a Fourier-bin spacing of 2 Hz. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 29, "date": "2026-09-24 21:52", "note": "Keep Section 2.2 introduction general; use moving window", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because normalization cancels in the ratios used below. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 30, "date": "2026-09-24 21:55", "note": "Clarify ratios that cancel spectral normalization", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The spectra are left unnormalized because their common scale cancels in the transfer-function estimate, coherence, and later power ratios. The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 31, "date": "2026-09-24 21:58", "note": "Remove unnecessary spectral normalization sentence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The frequency argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> is omitted for brevity. To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 32, "date": "2026-09-24 22:34", "note": "Port author deletion of frequency-argument sentence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also used to calculate coherence, which contributes to the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window keeps these spectra responsive to changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>At each update, the window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates are separated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> index the updates and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> index samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency at bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms give the instantaneous spectra</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between windows, the instantaneous spectra are averaged recursively with exponential weighting [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>], following the modified-periodogram approach of [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, the spectral averaging weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the 2–100 Hz band, excluding bins around the 60 Hz line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, but the stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 33, "date": "2026-09-24 22:37", "note": "Polish short-time spectral estimation prose and source attribution", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>The closed-loop transfer function is identified from the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> also enters the coherence estimate, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] averages modified periodograms of windowed records. To reduce variation between updates, the present method applies the geometric moving-average recurrence of Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] to the instantaneous spectra. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 34, "date": "2026-09-24 23:06", "note": "Connect spectral requirements and averaging evidence in Section 2.2", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Short-time spectral estimation</h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The instantaneous spectra are averaged to reduce variation between updates. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] derived the variance reduction from averaging modified periodograms under Gaussian and locally flat-spectrum assumptions. The present method applies the geometric moving-average recurrence of Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] to these spectra. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Direct identification branch</h2>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 35, "date": "2026-09-24 23:36", "note": "Merge spectral estimation and direct identification; clarify spectral citations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Control loop and stability margin</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 36, "date": "2026-09-25 09:47", "note": "Port author heading edit to Section 2.1", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>The loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 37, "date": "2026-09-25 09:48", "note": "Clarify transitions within Section 2.2", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this recursive averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>With these spectra, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 38, "date": "2026-09-25 09:49", "note": "Refine Section 2.2 transitional phrases", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 39, "date": "2026-09-25 12:59", "note": "Specify Welch per-window calculation and Equation 6 connection", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] calculates a modified periodogram from each windowed segment and extends the calculation to cross spectra. Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a> forms the corresponding power and cross spectra for each window. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 40, "date": "2026-09-25 13:13", "note": "Revert unapproved Welch wording to version 38", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1100 280\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure calculates the desired displacement d, which passes through the phase-lead compensator to give u. u enters a summing junction, the controller C, and the adaptive factor lambda, which produce the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The measured displacement y is fed back to the junction, and the measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a1\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"55\" width=\"140\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"210\" y=\"55\" width=\"130\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"490\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"640\" y=\"55\" width=\"110\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"810\" y=\"55\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"100\" r=\"18\"/>\n<path d=\"M150 100 H207\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M340 100 H409\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M448 100 H487\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M600 100 H637\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<path d=\"M750 100 H807\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M1010 100 H1092\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/><path d=\"M1045 100 V200 H430 V121\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n<path d=\"M910 145 V250 H80 V148\" stroke=\"#74058A\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 5\" marker-end=\"url(#a1)\"/>\n</g><circle cx=\"1045\" cy=\"100\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Numerical</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"275\" y=\"96\" class=\"nt\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"372\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"403\" y=\"84\" class=\"nt\">+</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"138\" class=\"nt\">−</text>\n<text x=\"545\" y=\"92\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" class=\"nt\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"695\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"779\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#AC6420\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"910\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"910\" y=\"134\" class=\"nt\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"1068\" y=\"86\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"700\" y=\"191\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#007CA8\">Measured actuator displacement</text>\n<text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" class=\"nt\" fill=\"#74058A\"><tspan class=\"it\">f</tspan><tspan class=\"it\" font-size=\"14\" dy=\"5\">m</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(t), measured force</tspan></text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 41, "date": "2026-09-25 13:45", "note": "new Figure 1: NCF OSI block, update arrow to the adaptive factor, d_m and f_m feedback", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction and to the NCF OSI. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure and to the NCF OSI. The NCF OSI updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"6\" y=\"95\" width=\"86\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"74\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"252\" y=\"95\" width=\"56\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"328\" y=\"95\" width=\"56\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"414\" y=\"95\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"420\" y=\"6\" width=\"82\" height=\"36\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M92 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M184 115 H212\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 115 H250\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M308 115 H326\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M384 115 H412\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M514 111 H592\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M540 111 V168 H222 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M540 111 V34 H504\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M514 124 H566 V190 H49 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M566 124 V14 H504\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M420 24 H356 V93\" stroke=\"#7D1F2D\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"540\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"566\" cy=\"124\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#A518AD\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"49\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"49\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"147\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"147\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"280\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"356\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"356\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"356\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"464\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"464\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"464\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"461\" y=\"28\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"197\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"233\" y=\"135\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"398\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"580\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"388\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7D1F2D\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 42, "date": "2026-09-25 13:46", "note": "Figure 1 spacing at true 9 pt", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction and to the NCF OSI. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure and to the NCF OSI. The NCF OSI updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"84\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"118\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"268\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"348\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"92\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"36\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M88 115 H116\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M198 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H266\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M326 115 H346\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M406 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V32 H522\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 124 H572 V190 H46 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M572 124 V14 H522\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M440 24 H377 V93\" stroke=\"#7D1F2D\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"572\" cy=\"124\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#A518AD\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"158\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"297\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"297\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"377\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"480\" y=\"28\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"228\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"423\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"408\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7D1F2D\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>The windowed power and cross spectra follow the segmentwise construction described by Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>]. They are averaged recursively to reduce variation between updates. For this averaging, Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives the geometric moving-average recurrence used here to weight recent windows more heavily as the specimen changes. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets the contribution of the new spectrum:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 43, "date": "2026-09-25 13:52", "note": "Apply approved Welch and Roberts wording", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction and to the NCF OSI. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure and to the NCF OSI. The NCF OSI updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"84\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"118\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"268\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"348\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"92\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"36\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M88 115 H116\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M198 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H266\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M326 115 H346\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M406 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V32 H522\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 124 H572 V190 H46 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M572 124 V14 H522\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M440 24 H377 V93\" stroke=\"#7D1F2D\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"572\" cy=\"124\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#A518AD\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"158\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"297\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"297\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"377\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"480\" y=\"28\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"228\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"423\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"408\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7D1F2D\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] describes calculating a modified periodogram for each record section after applying a data window and taking its Fourier transform. He extends the procedure to cross spectra. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 44, "date": "2026-09-25 16:45", "note": "Figure 1 update arrow in green #278A42", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction and to the NCF OSI. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure and to the NCF OSI. The NCF OSI updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"84\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"118\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"268\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"348\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"92\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"36\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M88 115 H116\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M198 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H266\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M326 115 H346\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M406 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V32 H522\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 124 H572 V190 H46 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M572 124 V14 H522\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M440 24 H377 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"572\" cy=\"124\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#A518AD\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"158\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"297\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"297\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"377\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"480\" y=\"28\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"228\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"423\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"408\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] describes calculating a modified periodogram for each record section after applying a data window and taking its Fourier transform. He extends the procedure to cross spectra. The transforms yield the instantaneous autospectra and cross spectrum</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 45, "date": "2026-09-26 01:12", "note": "Clarify Welch spectral construction and within-window spectra", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction and to the NCF OSI. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure and to the NCF OSI. The NCF OSI updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"84\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"118\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"268\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"348\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"92\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"80\" height=\"36\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M88 115 H116\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M198 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H266\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M326 115 H346\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M406 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M550 111 V32 H522\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M534 124 H572 V190 H46 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M572 124 V14 H522\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M440 24 H377 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"550\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"572\" cy=\"124\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#A518AD\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"46\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"46\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"158\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"158\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"297\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"297\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"377\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"377\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"480\" y=\"28\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"102\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"228\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"423\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"408\" y=\"19\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, respectively. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] obtains a power-spectrum estimate by averaging squared magnitudes of Fourier transforms from windowed data. For cross spectra, he averages products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. The calculations within each window are used here to form the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 46, "date": "2026-09-26 01:38", "note": "Figure 1: NCF OSI collects u(t) and d_m, f_m only to the numerical substructure, green OSI connections", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, respectively. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] obtains a power-spectrum estimate by averaging squared magnitudes of Fourier transforms from windowed data. For cross spectra, he averages products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. The calculations within each window are used here to form the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 47, "date": "2026-09-26 01:40", "note": "Add bridge paragraph after Equation (4) introducing the three branches", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, respectively. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] obtains a power-spectrum estimate by averaging squared magnitudes of Fourier transforms from windowed data. For cross spectra, he averages products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. The calculations within each window are used here to form the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 48, "date": "2026-09-26 01:50", "note": "Add NCF OSI block paragraph before Figure 1", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a href=\"#citation2\">2</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a href=\"#citation1\">1</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, respectively. Welch [<a href=\"#citation3\">3</a>] obtains a power-spectrum estimate by averaging squared magnitudes of Fourier transforms from windowed data. For cross spectra, he averages products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. The calculations within each window are used here to form the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a href=\"#citation4\">4</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a href=\"#citation6\">6</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a href=\"#citation5\">5</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"citation1\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation2\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"citation3\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation4\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation5\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n<li id=\"citation6\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 49, "date": "2026-09-26 01:58", "note": "Keyed auto-numbered citations, cite Blackman and Tukey for the Hann window", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>] and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, respectively. Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] obtains a power-spectrum estimate by averaging squared magnitudes of Fourier transforms from windowed data. For cross spectra, he averages products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. The calculations within each window are used here to form the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>To reduce variation between updates, these spectra are averaged recursively. Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>] gives a geometric moving-average recurrence that updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Applied to the spectra in Equation <a href=\"#eq6\">(6)</a>, the recurrence retains earlier spectra while their contributions decay as new windows arrive. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x\">doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 50, "date": "2026-09-26 02:17", "note": "Merge Welch and Roberts discussion, Google Scholar links for references", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>] and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i>Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/9.29424\">doi:10.1109/9.29424</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x\">doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901\">doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860\">doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</a></li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “Closed-loop identification revisited,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9\">doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</a></li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 51, "date": "2026-09-26 02:17", "note": "Reference titles link to Google Scholar searches", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>] and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Equation <a href=\"#eq5\">(5)</a> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> gives the Fourier transforms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(7)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (9)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (16)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13), (15)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(24)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (25)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (23) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equation <a href=\"#eq7\">(7)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq10\">(10)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 52, "date": "2026-09-26 02:31", "note": "Split lumped DFT, spectrum and averaging equations into separate equations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>r</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 53, "date": "2026-09-26 02:36", "note": "Hann window as displayed equation, fix escaped LaTeX", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (15)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 54, "date": "2026-09-26 02:36", "note": "Define complex conjugate superscript", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The indicator is then evaluated over the analysis set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, which excludes bins around the line-noise component:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>100</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>58</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>62</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> contains 47 bins. Subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (15)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 55, "date": "2026-09-26 02:40", "note": "State analysis set without implementation details", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. At update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the operating condition is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The specimen stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unknown and is not estimated.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 56, "date": "2026-09-26 02:50", "note": "Remove statement that stiffness is not estimated", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The adaptive factor at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>Because this estimate can be poor, each bin receives a confidence between zero and one. The confidence combines three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage. Even within an active window, some bins carry little command energy, and these bins are discounted through</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 57, "date": "2026-09-26 02:54", "note": "Explain why the identified estimate can be poor before the confidence factors", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The adaptive factor at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>This estimate is unbiased only if the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. In hybrid simulation, this condition is not guaranteed, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is computed from the measured force through the numerical substructure. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an unbiased estimate [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</p>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for three reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise, for example before or after the strong-motion phase of the ground motion. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. Third, measurement noise, nonlinear specimen response and the feedback noted above reduce the linear correlation between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors that address these causes. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-forssell\">7</a>].</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-forssell\">U. Forssell and L. Ljung, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Closed-loop+identification+revisited\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Closed-loop identification revisited</a>,” <i>Automatica</i>, vol. 35, no. 7, pp. 1215–1241, 1999. doi:10.1016/S0005-1098(99)00022-9</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 58, "date": "2026-09-26 02:55", "note": "Remove closed-loop bias paragraph and Forssell reference", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The adaptive factor at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for three reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise, for example before or after the strong-motion phase of the ground motion. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. Third, measurement noise and nonlinear specimen response reduce the linear correlation between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors that address these causes. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 59, "date": "2026-09-26 02:57", "note": "Two reasons for poor estimates, coherence as supporting evidence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The adaptive factor at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. High coherence is therefore treated as supporting evidence rather than proof of an accurate estimate.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 60, "date": "2026-09-26 03:00", "note": "Coherence sentence without duplicate therefore", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis, and subsequent expressions use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The adaptive factor at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. High coherence alone does not guarantee an accurate estimate, so it is treated as supporting evidence.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 61, "date": "2026-09-26 03:01", "note": "Port user edit: shorten analysis-set sentence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. High coherence alone does not guarantee an accurate estimate, so it is treated as supporting evidence.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the window RMS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>r</mi></msub><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 62, "date": "2026-09-26 03:04", "note": "Put window RMS directly in the activity equation", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information. High coherence alone does not guarantee an accurate estimate, so it is treated as supporting evidence.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 63, "date": "2026-09-26 03:05", "note": "Port user edit: drop coherence sentence", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>The threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is approximately <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4.54</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the command-noise standard deviation measured in a quiet record. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.0575</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold, recorded displacement units, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 64, "date": "2026-09-26 03:21", "note": "Activity threshold as eta_A times sigma_u", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which discounts bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 65, "date": "2026-09-26 03:23", "note": "Input leverage in positive voice", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which gives higher weight to bins where the command carries more energy relative to its strongest bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 66, "date": "2026-09-26 03:24", "note": "Input leverage: reduces the weight of low-energy bins", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0212C;</mi></mrow></math> is the full 2–100 Hz grid. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 67, "date": "2026-09-26 03:25", "note": "Normalize leverage over Omega", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 68, "date": "2026-09-26 03:25", "note": "Leverage equation punctuation", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (18), (20)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 69, "date": "2026-09-26 03:27", "note": "Relative power as its own equation before leverage", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The leverage then compares this relative power with the reference level <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 70, "date": "2026-09-26 03:28", "note": "Name leverage symbol in text", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The leverage then compares this relative power with the reference level <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 71, "date": "2026-09-26 03:32", "note": "Leverage: l_raw and l_floor for numerical stability", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 72, "date": "2026-09-26 03:34", "note": "Explain l_floor half-weight level", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. Where it is small, the estimate is replaced by the prior identification of the initial state, scaled by the current adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> at the initial stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the prior is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>Measurement and prior are blended in the closed-loop domain, where the spectral estimate is formed. The loop is then recovered by inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>As a result, a well-supported measurement governs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, while a weakly supported one leaves it close to the prior. The plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> then gives the identified and prior margin curves from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is a three-bin moving median over frequency that suppresses isolated spectral outliers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equations (19), (21)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 73, "date": "2026-09-26 03:41", "note": "ID section without prior blending, w_id and running median of three", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives a confidence between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the confidence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This confidence is a weighting factor, not a probability. It becomes the weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4. Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> recovers the identified loop and plant:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin curve then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the running median of three [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>]. It replaces each bin by the median of itself and its two neighbours, which removes isolated spectral outliers while preserving the trend of the curve.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the confidence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the three branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², c → b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (27)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1977.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 74, "date": "2026-09-26 03:47", "note": "Identification weight w_id replaces confidence c", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4. Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> recovers the identified loop and plant:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin curve then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the running median of three [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>]. It replaces each bin by the median of itself and its two neighbours, which removes isolated spectral outliers while preserving the trend of the curve.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(26)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (27)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (28)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (29) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq15\">(15)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq28\">(28)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1977.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 75, "date": "2026-09-26 03:49", "note": "Define b_id right after the H1 estimate, then its weight", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the plant estimate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> then gives the identified margin curve:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the running median of three [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>]. It replaces each bin by the median of itself and its two neighbours, which removes isolated spectral outliers while preserving the trend of the curve.</p>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(15)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (20)–(25)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (26)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (27)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(31)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equation (28) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq16\">(16)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq22\">(22)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq23\">(23)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1977.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 76, "date": "2026-09-26 03:52", "note": "One equation per display", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The SISO NCF stability margin for the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and scaled outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is defined as [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>inf</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>H</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the plant estimate</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>and Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a> then gives the identified margin curve:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the running median of three [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>]. It replaces each bin by the median of itself and its two neighbours, which removes isolated spectral outliers while preserving the trend of the curve.</p>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, the output-power margin curve uses the pointwise NCF margin from Equation <a href=\"#eq4\">(4)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>med</mo><mn>3</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (5)–(13)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (14)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (11)–(13)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1977.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 77, "date": "2026-09-26 04:14", "note": "b_NCF in terms of L, b_id as median of three most recent evaluations", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>] recommended smoothing by running medians. Here, the identified margin is the median of the three most recent evaluations of Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(20)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (21)–(27)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (28)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (29)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (33)–(34)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (20), (30) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq17\">(17)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq19\">(19)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq34\">(34)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, limited preliminary ed., vol. III, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1971.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 78, "date": "2026-09-26 04:20", "note": "Median of three most recent identified loops, then b_id from Equation (3)", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>] recommended smoothing by running medians. Here, the identified loop is smoothed by the median of its three most recent estimates:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, limited preliminary ed., vol. III, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1971.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 79, "date": "2026-09-26 04:45", "note": "Smoothed identified loop by median over neighbouring frequencies", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-tukey\">7</a>] recommended smoothing by running medians. Here, the identified loop is smoothed by the median over each frequency and its two neighbours:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-tukey\">J. W. Tukey, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Exploratory+Data+Analysis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Exploratory Data Analysis</a></i>, limited preliminary ed., vol. III, Reading, MA: Addison-Wesley, 1971.</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 80, "date": "2026-09-26 04:52", "note": "Cite Di Matteo et al. for neighbouring-frequency running median", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the Hann window [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 81, "date": "2026-09-26 11:16", "note": "Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight is not a probability. It sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 82, "date": "2026-09-26 11:16", "note": "Port user edit: drop 'is not a probability'", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak, yet a degrading specimen can still drive the loop toward instability during such periods. The measured output power offers a second source of evidence, because it reflects the loop even without strong excitation. This power is compared against a reference taken from the first three non-overlapping windows, which are assumed to represent the initial loop at full gain. The reference power and the observed ratio are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> is the transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> in reference window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. To interpret this ratio, assume that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is dominated by an equivalent output disturbance and that the spectrum of this disturbance is unchanged from the reference period. The output is then shaped by the sensitivity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the positive scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is an effective change in loop magnitude, not an estimate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. It is fitted in each bin by matching the observed and predicted ratios on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> holds 25 logarithmically spaced values from 0.5 to 6. Because each bin is fitted independently, the curve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an inference model rather than an identified transfer function. A single fit is noisy, and the excitation may fade after a change is detected. The fitted change is therefore retained in a logarithmic memory initialized at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>While the command is active (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), the target is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The memory releases a change once the loop recovers, and recovery is detected from the band-median ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The memory then decays toward <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaced by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Otherwise, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is held, so a detected change remains represented while the excitation is weak. Finally, Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> gives the output-power margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The output-power branch is trusted in proportion to the size of the inferred change,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>which rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, this detection weight sets the weights of the other two branches at each frequency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ḡ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(28)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (29)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (30)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (31) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq26\">(26)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 83, "date": "2026-09-26 13:31", "note": "Section 2.3 polished: g-hat notation, branch weight moved in, recovery last", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>However, a small inferred amplification may only reflect the resolution of the grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math>. The amplification is therefore trusted in proportion to its size:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>clip</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> limits its argument to the interval from zero to one, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the detection scale. The detection weight thus rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects only the current spectra, the smoothed amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is held while the command is inactive. It would therefore keep reporting a change after the output power has returned to its initial level. A recovery criterion is thus needed. For this purpose, the observed power ratio is monitored across the analysis band:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the band median of the observed power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in the analysis. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The smoothed amplification then decays toward one:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ĝ</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (31)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (32)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Detection scale, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 84, "date": "2026-09-26 13:44", "note": "Detection weight with threshold g_th and [0,1] condition", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects only the current spectra, the smoothed amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is held while the command is inactive. It would therefore keep reporting a change after the output power has returned to its initial level. A recovery criterion is thus needed. For this purpose, the observed power ratio is monitored across the analysis band:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the band median of the observed power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in the analysis. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the output power has fallen below the initial reference. The smoothed amplification then decays toward one:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ĝ</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(30)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (31)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (32)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (34)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (32) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.27</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Output-power memory, recovery, Equation <a href=\"#eq30\">(30)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 85, "date": "2026-09-26 13:54", "note": "Recovery step simplified with recovery factor r_g", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>These weights are nonnegative and sum to one. Direct identification receives the share supported by the measurement. The output-power branch receives the remainder in proportion to its detection weight, and the prior receives what neither measurement supports (Table 2). The fused margin curve is therefore</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ĝ</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (33)–(34)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq34\">(34)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab2\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Evidence sources fused in Equation <a href=\"#eq32\">(32)</a>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Evidence</th><th>Weight</th><th>Governs in</th><th>Principal assumption</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Direct identification</td><td>Cross-spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>Active, coherent, well-excited bins</td><td>Disturbance uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Output-power inference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> relative to initial reference</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi></mrow></math></td><td>Weak excitation after a detected change</td><td>Stationary disturbance and a magnitude-only change</td></tr><tr><td>Initial-state prior</td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>C</mi></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></td><td>No measured evidence</td><td>Loop unchanged from the initial state</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 86, "date": "2026-09-26 13:57", "note": "Remove weight-summary paragraph and Table 2", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The instantaneous indicator is the second-smallest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the governing frequency. Discarding the lowest bin prevents a single outlier from governing the estimate, at the cost of attenuating a genuine minimum that is only one bin wide. To suppress fluctuations between updates, the reported indicator is smoothed in time,</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>and the adaptive law uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, reduced by the second-smallest value over the analysis band, and smoothed to give b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">T̂, γ², w<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ĝ</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">H<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λC → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Trim and smooth</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equations (33)–(34)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Indicator smoothing, Equation <a href=\"#eq34\">(34)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation, trimming, and temporal smoothing mean that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The reported indicator also follows abrupt changes with a delay set by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 87, "date": "2026-09-26 14:05", "note": "b_OSI as minimum of fused curve over Omega; Figure 2, Table 1, A4 updated", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 480\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed the spectral estimator. Its spectra feed the identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior scaled by lambda. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"56\" y=\"150\" width=\"208\" height=\"100\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"280\" width=\"280\" height=\"90\"/>\n<rect x=\"680\" y=\"150\" width=\"180\" height=\"100\"/><rect x=\"680\" y=\"300\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\"/>\n<rect x=\"320\" y=\"400\" width=\"280\" height=\"56\"/>\n<path d=\"M10 180 H54\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M264 200 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M285 200 V75 H318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M600 75 H640 V180 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 200 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M600 325 H640 V220 H678\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M770 250 V298\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M860 335 H988\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M930 335 V428 H602\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><path d=\"M460 400 V372\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g><path d=\"M10 220 H54\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"200\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"930\" cy=\"335\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"currentColor\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"28\" y=\"172\" class=\"it\">u</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" class=\"it\" fill=\"#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"160\" y=\"190\">Windowed DFT,</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"214\">averaged spectra</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"64\">Direct identification</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"90\" class=\"nt\">L̂<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, w</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"112\" class=\"nt\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"189\">Output-power inference</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">q<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, ĝ</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\"> → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"237\" class=\"nt\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"314\">Initial-state prior</text><text x=\"460\" y=\"340\" class=\"nt\">L<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">, λ → b</tspan><tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-4\">(ω)</tspan></text><text x=\"460\" y=\"362\" class=\"nt\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"190\">Per-bin fusion</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"215\" class=\"nt\">β(ω)</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"238\" class=\"nt\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"770\" y=\"330\">Minimum over Ω</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"356\" class=\"nt\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"460\" y=\"434\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"620\" y=\"66\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">id</tspan></text><text x=\"624\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">pow</tspan></text><text x=\"620\" y=\"316\" class=\"it\">w<tspan font-size=\"14\" dy=\"4\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"958\" y=\"322\" class=\"nt\">b<tspan font-size=\"13\" dy=\"4\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"484\" y=\"392\" class=\"it\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives b<sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor also enters the initial-state prior and the output-power model.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 88, "date": "2026-09-26 14:14", "note": "Figure 2 redesign: aligned rows, symbols, lambda bus to all branches", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 510\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#AC6420\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"455\" width=\"270\" height=\"44\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<path d=\"M2 205 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 230 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 230 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V205 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V255 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V477 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 255 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#AC6420\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<path d=\"M340 477 H310 V120\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 120 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 265 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 410 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"230\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"265\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#AC6420\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"410\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#AC6420\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"190\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"240\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"477\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"294\" y=\"447\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#AC6420\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (copper) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 89, "date": "2026-09-26 14:31", "note": "Figure 2: green adaptive path, arrows at 1/3, 2/3 and middle", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 510\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green together with the adaptive law, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#39BF5E\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#39BF5E\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"455\" width=\"270\" height=\"44\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V477 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 477 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#39BF5E\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#39BF5E\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"477\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"294\" y=\"447\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#39BF5E\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 90, "date": "2026-09-26 14:32", "note": "Figure 2: darker green strokes, light green adaptive-law fill", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 510\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green together with the adaptive law, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which drives the adaptive law that returns lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"455\" width=\"270\" height=\"44\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V477 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 477 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"477\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive law</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"294\" y=\"447\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#2A9A48\">λ</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 91, "date": "2026-09-26 14:33", "note": "Figure 2: block renamed Adaptive factor lambda", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 510\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"455\" width=\"270\" height=\"44\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V477 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 477 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"477\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor <tspan class=\"it\">λ</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 92, "date": "2026-09-26 14:34", "note": "Figure 2: lambda on second line of adaptive-factor block", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 530\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"450\" width=\"270\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V485 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 485 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"471\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"499\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 93, "date": "2026-09-26 14:35", "note": "Figure 2: Equation (xx) placeholder in adaptive-factor block", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 555\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"445\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V495 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 495 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"467\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"495\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"523\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (xx)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 94, "date": "2026-09-26 14:35", "note": "Figure 2: even spacing for adaptive-factor block", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (xx)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> One estimator update. Branch weights from Equations (21), (28) and (31) are marked on the arrows entering the fusion block. The minimum of the fused curve over Ω gives <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>, and the adaptive factor <i>λ</i> (green) enters all three branches.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 95, "date": "2026-09-26 14:41", "note": "Revise Figure 2 caption", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (xx)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 96, "date": "2026-09-26 19:03", "note": "Add adaptive factor opening", "html": "\n<h1>2. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 2.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (xx)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 2.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 97, "date": "2026-09-26 19:12", "note": "Section 3.5 adaptive factor equations; headings renumbered to Section 3", "html": "\n<h1>3. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">3.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 98, "date": "2026-09-26 19:14", "note": "Move Section 3.5 before Figure 2", "html": "\n<h1>3. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds all three branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V66.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H304 A6 6 0 0 1 316 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V103.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 103.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"248.3\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">3.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 99, "date": "2026-09-26 19:16", "note": "Figure 2: lambda feeds only output-power and prior branches", "html": "\n<h1>3. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Ref. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">3.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">M. Gomaa, E. D. Patino, J. N. Villamizar, J. W. Condori Uribe, and S. J. Dyke, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">J. S. Bendat and A. G. Piersol, <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Hoboken, NJ: Wiley, 2010.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">K. Glover and D. McFarlane, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, vol. 34, no. 8, pp. 821–830, 1989. doi:10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">R. B. Blackman and J. W. Tukey, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, vol. 37, no. 1, pp. 185–282, 1958. doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">P. D. Welch, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, vol. 15, no. 2, pp. 70–73, 1967. doi:10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">S. W. Roberts, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, vol. 1, no. 3, pp. 239–250, 1959. doi:10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">S. Di Matteo, N. M. Viall, and L. Kepko, “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, vol. 126, no. 2, e2020JA028748, 2021. doi:10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 100, "date": "2026-09-26 20:07", "note": "Author-year citations; Wu and Wang (2020) in Section 3.3", "html": "\n<h1>3. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">Gomaa et al. (in press)</a>.</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">Gomaa et al. (in press)</a>, quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">Bendat and Piersol 2010</a>). The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">Glover and McFarlane 1989</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">Blackman and Tukey (1958)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">Welch (1967)</a> proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">Roberts (1959)</a>, which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">Bendat and Piersol 2010</a>) and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo et al. (2021)</a>, so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">Wu and Wang (2020)</a>, who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">Gomaa et al. (in press)</a> and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">3.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 101, "date": "2026-09-26 20:08", "note": "Numbered citations by first appearance, new reference format", "html": "\n<h1>3. Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 1, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 1 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.4.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.3</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">8</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section xx, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.4</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 2 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 1 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.5</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.4 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s6\"><span class=\"secno\">3.6</span>Assumptions and limitations</h2>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 102, "date": "2026-10-01 14:16", "note": "Add Section 2 problem formulation and Section 3.2 calibration, renumber 3.1.x, move assumptions to Appendix A", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Section draft rewritten from manuscript2 for journal submission. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears with a physical specimen.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>To examine the outer controller loop alone, the tests in this section exclude the numerical substructure. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then a prescribed displacement instead of a computed response. Consequently, the measured force is not fed back, and only the outer controller loop remains closed. The specimen is [description: column, material, dimensions], tested at [laboratory]. A [actuator model] imposes the lateral displacement at [location], and [sensor] measures it. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> runs on a Speedgoat real-time target at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. In the damaged state, [how the damage was introduced] reduced the lateral stiffness to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Both stiffness values were estimated from the measured force and displacement [method]. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, [excitation] was applied through the outer controller loop with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Because both loops use the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, any difference between them comes from the specimen. Figure 1 compares the open-loop frequency responses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Below 10 Hz, the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> is about 2.4 times that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. However, the phases of the two loops differ by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly raises the loop gain rather than adding phase lag.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><div style=\"border:1px dashed currentColor;padding:2.5em 1em;text-align:center;opacity:.7\">[Open-loop Bode plot of L<sub>0</sub> and L<sub>d</sub>, 0.5–100 Hz, with the NCF chordal curve. To be generated.]</div><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Open-loop frequency responses of the outer controller loop for the initial and damaged systems.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Table 1 lists the resulting margins. The gain margin falls from 16.4 dB to 6.9 dB, whereas the phase margin stays near 87°. Consequently, the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. This is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Stability margins of the outer controller loop for the initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></th><th>Damaged, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr><tr><td>Gain margin</td><td>16.4 dB (at 235 Hz)</td><td>6.9 dB (at 252 Hz)</td></tr><tr><td>Phase margin</td><td>88.1°</td><td>86.8°</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Experimental instability</h2>\n<p>[Pending confirmation of the record.] The consequence of this reduction was observed in a hybrid simulation with the damaged specimen. Figure 2 shows [measured versus computed displacement, El Centro scaled by 0.04, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>].</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_unstable\"><div style=\"border:1px dashed currentColor;padding:2.5em 1em;text-align:center;opacity:.7\">[Hybrid simulation with the damaged specimen that became unstable. Record to be confirmed.]</div><figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Hybrid simulation with the damaged specimen.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s26\"><span class=\"secno\">2.6</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section discusses the framework evaluation in detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 3, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 3 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">3</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">5</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">6</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">2</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">7</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">8</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 4 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains [13] hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin. Table 2 lists them with their calibrated values.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.55</td><td>—</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Sudden damage is a step in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one and back. Gradual damage is a ramp down and back up. The second feature is the excitation band. Broadband excitation covers 2 to 100 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Low-frequency excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it.</p>\n<p>The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Broadband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Broadband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Low-frequency, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Low-frequency, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Broadband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Broadband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Low-frequency, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Low-frequency, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figure 5 shows each case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_calib\"><div style=\"border:1px dashed currentColor;padding:2.5em 1em;text-align:center;opacity:.7\">[Three panels per case: (a) command, copper, and measured displacement, blue; (b) damage index δ; (c) b<sub>OSI</sub>, true margin, b<sub>tar</sub> and λ. Runs pending in the paper worktree.]</div><figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. [Map each case to the parameters it sets, for example the return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and the onset of damage sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>.] The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 103, "date": "2026-10-01 16:16", "note": "Revise Section 2 per v102 comments, add figures, paper benchmark results, design vs calibrated parameters, REFORM citation", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the frame. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of [10 kip] imposes the lateral displacement, and [sensor] measures it. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. The base then loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 520 125\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"160\" y=\"35\" width=\"70\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"290\" y=\"35\" width=\"110\" height=\"40\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"55\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M20 55 H100\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M118 55 H158\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 55 H288\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M400 55 H500\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 55 V105 H110 V65\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"450\" cy=\"55\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"195\" y=\"52\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"65\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"345\" y=\"46\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"59\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"71\">and specimen</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"55\" y=\"48\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text><text x=\"97\" y=\"45\">+</text><text x=\"101\" y=\"78\">−</text>\n<text x=\"259\" y=\"48\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text><text x=\"485\" y=\"48\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Both stiffness values were estimated from the measured force and displacement by windowed total least squares. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, [excitation] was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cl\"><img src=\"figures/fig_closed_loop.svg\" alt=\"Closed-loop magnitude and phase for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3 (alternative).</b> Closed-loop frequency response from <i>u</i> to <i>y</i> for the initial and damaged systems. [Author to choose between this and the open-loop version.]</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 13 parameters, listed in Table 2. Six of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and the window length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> set the frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The upper bound covers the frequencies of the minimum margin. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining seven are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–100 Hz, 47 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference is band-limited random noise with an RMS of 0.15, and white noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 is added to the measurement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 100 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–100 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.017</td><td>3.2</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.013</td><td>3.4</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.018</td><td>0.005</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.026</td><td>0.73</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.015</td><td>3.3</td><td>0.75</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.012</td><td>3.4</td><td>0.61</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.018</td><td>0.005</td><td>1.67</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.030</td><td>0.72</td><td>2.13</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 104, "date": "2026-10-01 16:45", "note": "Setup facts, breakout wording, redrawn loop figure, TLS citation, Omega 2-80 Hz, paper-formulation benchmark results, unsafe-time metric", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. These stiffness values quantify the damage in each state and are not used by the framework. Both were estimated from the measured force and displacement by windowed total least squares. The measured force and displacement both contain noise. Markovsky and Van Huffel [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-markovsky\">3</a>] noted that ordinary least squares is then inconsistent, whereas total least squares gives a consistent estimate. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">4</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">6</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">7</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">8</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">4</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">9</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">10</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 13 parameters, listed in Table 2. Six of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining seven are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-markovsky\">Markovsky, I., and Van Huffel, S. (2007), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Overview+of+total+least-squares+methods\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Overview of total least-squares methods</a>,” <i>Signal Processing</i>, 87(10), 2283–2302. DOI: 10.1016/j.sigpro.2007.04.004</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 105, "date": "2026-10-01 17:08", "note": "Remove stiffness estimation method and TLS citation from Section 2.3", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum recorded in the initial state before the excitation, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The selected amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive selections are averaged recursively, as for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), with more weight on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest selection. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin. The adaptive factor is then updated multiplicatively:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math> is the adaptation gain, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> decreases while the margin is below the target and recovers toward one once spare margin returns. The multiplicative form keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> positive and makes each change proportional to its current value. The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> restores the full outer controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(29)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (30)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (31)–(32)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (35)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 13 parameters, listed in Table 2. Six of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining seven are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (34)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (35)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq27\">(27)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 106, "date": "2026-10-01 22:19", "note": "Apply Ref+gate+up: three-snapshot reference, amplification confirmation, and slower adaptive-factor recovery", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msqrt><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>To ensure numerical stability for low values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is introduced in the final expression of the leverage:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets the relative power at which a bin receives half weight. Bins well above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> receive nearly full weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state. The averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>.</p>\n<p>The output-power branch detects a change by comparing the present output power with a reference from the initial state. A single window of output power fluctuates strongly, so a reference taken from one window could bias every later comparison. The reference autospectrum is therefore the median of three raw autospectra recorded in the initial state. These are taken at 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 s, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>med</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> are the corresponding updates. The median discards one unusually loud or quiet window.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At frequencies without excitation, the output consists mainly of sensor noise, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> can indicate an amplification that does not exist. At the excited frequencies, however, the loop is identified directly, and degradation appears as an increase in its magnitude. Section 2.4 observed such an increase by a factor of about 2.4. An amplification inferred from output power is therefore accepted only when the identified loop also shows an increase.</p>\n<p>This increase is measured by the real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> that best scales the prior loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> to the identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The factor is fitted in the weighted least-squares sense:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>Re</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>The weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> lets the well-identified excited frequencies dominate. The identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is taken before frequency-median smoothing. The factor is real, consistent with the real amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> assumed above, so only the real part of the numerator is retained. The denominator is real already. For an unchanged specimen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, whereas for the damaged specimen of Section 2 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> then sets how much of the inferred amplification is accepted:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Accordingly, none of the inferred amplification is accepted when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and all of it is accepted once the loop is 50% larger. The accepted amplification is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the averaging equation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>. Without confirmation, 10% of the inferred amplification is still accepted while the command is active. A persistent change can therefore accumulate slowly, whereas a brief noise burst cannot.</p>\n<p>The accepted amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive accepted amplifications are averaged recursively. As for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), more weight is placed on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest accepted amplification. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin.</p>\n<p>Reducing and restoring the adaptive factor carry different risks. A slow reduction leaves the loop exposed while the margin is insufficient. Conversely, a fast recovery can raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> back toward instability before the estimate settles. The adaptive factor is therefore reduced and restored at different rates:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow></math> is the ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> falls at full rate when the margin is below the target and recovers about three times more slowly. The adaptation gain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (34)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (35)–(36)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (37)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (39)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 15 parameters, listed in Table 2. Seven of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining eight are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.1</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Share of the inferred amplification accepted without confirmation</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (38)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.32</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 107, "date": "2026-10-12 23:41", "note": "Explain why leverage needs l_floor, write activity test with RMS", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the root mean square of the command samples in the considered window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>The raw leverage cannot serve as the weight directly. It is proportional to the bin power, so a bin with one tenth of the peak power would receive one tenth of the weight even though its estimate is still reliable. The weight should instead stay close to one while a bin carries enough command power and fall toward zero only when it carries too little. The hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets this boundary:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>which stays close to one in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and decreases smoothly toward zero in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226A;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. A bin with command power equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> times the peak receives half weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state. The averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>.</p>\n<p>The output-power branch detects a change by comparing the present output power with a reference from the initial state. A single window of output power fluctuates strongly, so a reference taken from one window could bias every later comparison. The reference autospectrum is therefore the median of three raw autospectra recorded in the initial state. These are taken at 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 s, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>med</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> are the corresponding updates. The median discards one unusually loud or quiet window.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation is assumed to be an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At frequencies without excitation, the output consists mainly of sensor noise, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> can indicate an amplification that does not exist. At the excited frequencies, however, the loop is identified directly, and degradation appears as an increase in its magnitude. Section 2.4 observed such an increase by a factor of about 2.4. An amplification inferred from output power is therefore accepted only when the identified loop also shows an increase.</p>\n<p>This increase is measured by the real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> that best scales the prior loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> to the identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The factor is fitted in the weighted least-squares sense:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>Re</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>The weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> lets the well-identified excited frequencies dominate. The identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is taken before frequency-median smoothing. The factor is real, consistent with the real amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> assumed above, so only the real part of the numerator is retained. The denominator is real already. For an unchanged specimen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, whereas for the damaged specimen of Section 2 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> then sets how much of the inferred amplification is accepted:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Accordingly, none of the inferred amplification is accepted when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and all of it is accepted once the loop is 50% larger. The accepted amplification is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the averaging equation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>. Without confirmation, 10% of the inferred amplification is still accepted while the command is active. A persistent change can therefore accumulate slowly, whereas a brief noise burst cannot.</p>\n<p>The accepted amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive accepted amplifications are averaged recursively. As for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), more weight is placed on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest accepted amplification. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin.</p>\n<p>Reducing and restoring the adaptive factor carry different risks. A slow reduction leaves the loop exposed while the margin is insufficient. Conversely, a fast recovery can raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> back toward instability before the estimate settles. The adaptive factor is therefore reduced and restored at different rates:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow></math> is the ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> falls at full rate when the margin is below the target and recovers about three times more slowly. The adaptation gain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (34)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (35)–(36)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (37)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (39)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 15 parameters, listed in Table 2. Seven of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining eight are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.1</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Share of the inferred amplification accepted without confirmation</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (38)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.32</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 108, "date": "2026-10-12 23:44", "note": "Answer v107 comments: define p_yy,0, represent degradation by g, rename q_mod to q_model", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the root mean square of the command samples in the considered window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>The raw leverage cannot serve as the weight directly. It is proportional to the bin power, so a bin with one tenth of the peak power would receive one tenth of the weight even though its estimate is still reliable. The weight should instead stay close to one while a bin carries enough command power and fall toward zero only when it carries too little. The hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets this boundary:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>which stays close to one in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and decreases smoothly toward zero in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226A;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. A bin with command power equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> times the peak receives half weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state. The averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> recorded in the initial state.</p>\n<p>The output-power branch detects a change by comparing the present output power with a reference from the initial state. A single window of output power fluctuates strongly, so a reference taken from one window could bias every later comparison. The reference autospectrum is therefore the median of three raw autospectra recorded in the initial state. These are taken at 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 s, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>med</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> are the corresponding updates. The median discards one unusually loud or quiet window.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation can be represented by an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>At frequencies without excitation, the output consists mainly of sensor noise, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> can indicate an amplification that does not exist. At the excited frequencies, however, the loop is identified directly, and degradation appears as an increase in its magnitude. Section 2.4 observed such an increase by a factor of about 2.4. An amplification inferred from output power is therefore accepted only when the identified loop also shows an increase.</p>\n<p>This increase is measured by the real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> that best scales the prior loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> to the identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The factor is fitted in the weighted least-squares sense:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>Re</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>The weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> lets the well-identified excited frequencies dominate. The identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is taken before frequency-median smoothing. The factor is real, consistent with the real amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> assumed above, so only the real part of the numerator is retained. The denominator is real already. For an unchanged specimen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, whereas for the damaged specimen of Section 2 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> then sets how much of the inferred amplification is accepted:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Accordingly, none of the inferred amplification is accepted when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and all of it is accepted once the loop is 50% larger. The accepted amplification is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the averaging equation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a>. Without confirmation, 10% of the inferred amplification is still accepted while the command is active. A persistent change can therefore accumulate slowly, whereas a brief noise burst cannot.</p>\n<p>The accepted amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive accepted amplifications are averaged recursively. As for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), more weight is placed on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest accepted amplification. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin.</p>\n<p>Reducing and restoring the adaptive factor carry different risks. A slow reduction leaves the loop exposed while the margin is insufficient. Conversely, a fast recovery can raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> back toward instability before the estimate settles. The adaptive factor is therefore reduced and restored at different rates:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow></math> is the ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> falls at full rate when the margin is below the target and recovers about three times more slowly. The adaptation gain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(33)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (34)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (35)–(36)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (37)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (39)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 15 parameters, listed in Table 2. Seven of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining eight are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.1</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Share of the inferred amplification accepted without confirmation</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq29\">(29)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (38)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.32</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (39)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}, {"v": 109, "date": "2026-10-13 01:31", "note": "Section 3.1.2: why false amplification, why rho, least-squares derivation, c_n with rho_th and floor epsilon", "html": "\n<p class=\"meta\">Manuscript draft. Readers are assumed to be familiar with Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>].</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec2\"><span class=\"secno\">2</span>Problem formulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"s21\"><span class=\"secno\">2.1</span>Motivation</h2>\n<p>Hybrid simulation relies on the transfer system to impose the computed displacements on the specimen. When the outer controller loop loses stability, the actuator drives the specimen far beyond its intended response. Such instability creates safety hazards for laboratory personnel. In addition, it can damage the specimen and expensive equipment such as the actuator and servo-valve. An unstable test is also usually terminated early, so the experiment fails and must be repeated with a new specimen. Maghareh et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-maghareh\">2</a>] noted that a failure in part of the physical substructure can drastically change its dynamics. They also noted that such a change affects the safety and integrity of the experiment.</p>\n<p>Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] showed that specimen degradation can cause this instability. As the specimen degrades, its stiffness decreases and the same control action produces a larger response. The resulting gain amplification reduces the stability margin of the outer controller loop. However, that finding was obtained from a virtual hybrid simulation. This section therefore examines whether the same mechanism appears in a physical experiment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s22\"><span class=\"secno\">2.2</span>Experimental setup</h2>\n<p>Figure 1 shows the experimental setup. The specimen is a steel column. Its base is anchored to a concrete block with four Hilti anchors, and its top is connected to the actuator. An MTS hydraulic actuator with a capacity of 10 kip imposes the lateral displacement at the top of the column. The internal LVDT of the actuator measures this displacement. A Speedgoat real-time target sends the command to the actuator at 2048 Hz.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_setup\"><img src=\"figures/fig_setup.jpg\" alt=\"Steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by an MTS actuator\" style=\"width:60%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> Experimental setup: steel column specimen anchored to a concrete block and loaded laterally by a hydraulic actuator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Damage is introduced through the anchorage. As the specimen is pushed, the concrete around the anchors cracks. This cracking is an early stage of concrete breakout. The breakout cone did not form, and the cracks were not yet visible, so the damage is partial. The base nevertheless loses part of its rotational stiffness. Consequently, the lateral stiffness at the actuator control point decreases.</p>\n<p>No numerical substructure is involved in the tests of this section. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a prescribed displacement, so only the outer controller loop is closed. Figure 2 shows this loop. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> acts on the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and sends the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is then fed back to the controller.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_loop\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"flt fld\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 130\">\n<title id=\"flt\">Outer controller loop used in the tests of Section 2</title>\n<desc id=\"fld\">The reference u enters a summing junction, where the measured displacement y is subtracted. The tracking error enters the controller C(s), which sends the command v to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k). The output y is fed back to the summing junction.</desc>\n<style>#fig_loop svg text{font-size:13px}#fig_loop svg .lb{font-size:14px}</style>\n<defs><marker id=\"fla\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"><rect x=\"170\" y=\"30\" width=\"90\" height=\"46\"/><rect x=\"320\" y=\"30\" width=\"130\" height=\"46\"/></g>\n<circle cx=\"115\" cy=\"53\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.1\">\n<path d=\"M30 53 H104\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M124 53 H168\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M260 53 H318\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M450 53 H540\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n<path d=\"M495 53 V110 H115 V64\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#fla)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"495\" cy=\"53\" r=\"2.4\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"50\" class=\"it lb\">C(s)</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"66\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"385\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"60\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"385\" y=\"73\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">u(t)</text><text x=\"102\" y=\"42\">+</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"80\">−</text>\n<text x=\"289\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">v(t)</text><text x=\"525\" y=\"45\" class=\"it lb\">y(t)</text>\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Outer controller loop used in the tests of this section.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s23\"><span class=\"secno\">2.3</span>Initial and damaged systems</h2>\n<p>The plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was identified in two specimen states. In the initial state, the lateral stiffness of the specimen was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7.20</mn></mrow></math> MN/m. After the anchorage was damaged, the lateral stiffness fell to 5.38 MN/m, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The two plants are denoted <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> hereafter.</p>\n<p>In each state, band-limited white noise up to 80 Hz was applied through the loop of Figure 2 with the same <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The transfer function from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> was then estimated between 0.1 and 80 Hz. Frequencies with coherence below 0.8 were excluded. A parametric model was fitted to each estimate, with five poles for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and six for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s24\"><span class=\"secno\">2.4</span>Effect of degradation on stability</h2>\n<p>Consider again the loop of Figure 2. The controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the same in both states, and only the specimen changes. The open-loop transfer function is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the damaged state. Figure 3 compares the two frequency responses and the chordal distance used by the NCF margin.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_ol\"><img src=\"figures/fig_open_loop.svg\" alt=\"Open-loop magnitude, phase and chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems\" style=\"width:70%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Open-loop frequency response of the outer controller loop and its chordal distance for the initial and damaged systems. Markers show the minima, and the dotted line shows the 0.4 guideline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The 25% loss of lateral stiffness raised the loop gain by a factor of about 2.4 below 10 Hz. However, the phase of the loop changed by less than 4° in this range. The loss of stiffness therefore mainly amplifies the loop gain rather than adding phase lag. Consequently, the chordal distance dips near 35–38 Hz, and the NCF margin falls from 0.59 to 0.34. Table 1 summarizes the two states. The damaged margin is below the 0.4 guideline recommended by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. These trends agree with the gain-amplification mechanism reported in [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>]. They also support the assumption in Section 3.1.2 that degradation scales the open-loop gain by a real factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_margins\"><figcaption><b>Table 1.</b> Initial and damaged systems.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Initial</th><th>Damaged</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lateral stiffness</td><td>7.20 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td><td>5.38 MN/m, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.75</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>Loop gain below 10 Hz</td><td>1</td><td>about 2.4</td></tr><tr><td>NCF margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.59 (at 35 Hz)</td><td>0.34 (at 38 Hz)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s25\"><span class=\"secno\">2.5</span>Problem statement</h2>\n<p>These results expose a dilemma for a fixed outer controller. A controller tuned for the initial state becomes too aggressive once the specimen degrades. Conversely, a controller tuned for the damaged state sacrifices tracking in the initial state. The specimen state is also unknown during the experiment. The stability of the outer controller loop must therefore be quantified online from the available measurements. The controller must then be updated accordingly so the experiment is completed safely and successfully. Section 3 presents a framework that meets both requirements.</p>\n<h1 id=\"sec3\"><span class=\"secno\">3</span>Online NCF stability indicator framework for adaptive control</h1>\n<p class=\"lead\">The NCF online stability indicator (NCF OSI), proposed by Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], quantifies the robustness of the closed-loop system as the physical plant changes during the experiment. The study showed that specimen stiffness degradation can drive the outer controller loop toward instability through a gain-amplification mechanism. In this study, we extend the analytical results to experimental implementation, discuss the challenges that arise, and propose ways to address them. The proposed framework comprises two parts: an online estimator of the indicator and an adaptive controller that gets updated accordingly. The indicator is estimated during the experiment from the desired displacement calculated by the numerical substructure and the measured actuator displacement by combining three estimator branches at each frequency, each weighted by the confidence in its output. The first is direct identification, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which estimates the closed-loop transfer function of the outer controller loop from the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> cross-spectral estimator [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>]. The second is output-power inference, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which can detect loop amplification at frequencies that cannot be reliably identified. This branch compares the output power measured during the experiment against the reference recorded before the excitation. The third is the prior identification of the initial state, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, which serves as a fallback when insufficient information is available. The adaptive controller then uses the estimated indicator to scale the controller output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, keeping the loop stable with an adequate margin as the specimen degrades while retaining as much tracking performance as possible. This section presents the framework and then its calibration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s1\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1</span>Framework Architecture</h2>\n<p>In the hybrid simulation block diagram shown in Figure 4, the numerical substructure computes the desired displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the ground acceleration and measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. To compensate for transfer-system lag and improve tracking, a phase-lead compensator receives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and produces the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The outer controller <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> takes the tracking error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the measured actuator displacement. It scales its output by the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> to produce the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> sent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The plant comprises the transfer system and the specimen of stiffness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. The transfer system includes the inner-loop controller, servo-valve, and actuator. The control–structure interaction between the actuator and specimen is also included in the transfer-system dynamics. The measured force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the numerical substructure and closes the numerical substructure loop, while the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is fed back to the outer controller, closing the outer-controller loop.</p>\n<p>The NCF OSI block runs alongside the outer-controller loop and does not alter the signals within it. It collects the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the measured displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over a moving window and estimates the NCF margin of the current loop at each update. The adaptive law then compares this estimate with a target margin and updates the adaptive factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. A lower margin reduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> to restore stability, while a sufficient margin lets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> recover toward one to retain tracking performance.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig1\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f1t f1d\" viewBox=\"0 0 602 200\" data-units=\"px\">\n<title id=\"f1t\">Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup</title>\n<desc id=\"f1d\">The numerical substructure computes the desired displacement d, which the phase-lead compensator turns into the reference u. The tracking error u minus d_m enters the controller C(s). The adaptive factor lambda scales its output to give the command v applied to the transfer system and specimen H(s,k), whose output is y. The measured displacement d_m returns to the summing junction. The measured force f_m returns to the numerical substructure. The NCF OSI collects the reference u and the measured displacement d_m and updates lambda.</desc>\n<defs><marker id=\"f1a\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"context-stroke\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"95\" width=\"82\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"110\" y=\"95\" width=\"78\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"270\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"352\" y=\"95\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\"/><rect x=\"442\" y=\"95\" width=\"94\" height=\"40\"/>\n<rect x=\"440\" y=\"6\" width=\"96\" height=\"34\" fill=\"#BEDCC6\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"238\" cy=\"115\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1\">\n<path d=\"M86 115 H108\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M188 115 H228\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M246 115 H268\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M328 115 H350\" stroke=\"#404040\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M410 115 H440\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.3\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 111 H600\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V168 H238 V125\" stroke=\"#1689BA\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M536 124 H574 V190 H45 V137\" stroke=\"#A518AD\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M216 115 V23 H438\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M556 111 V23 H538\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n<path d=\"M470 40 V62 H381 V93\" stroke=\"#278A42\" marker-end=\"url(#f1a)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"556\" cy=\"111\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#1689BA\"/><circle cx=\"216\" cy=\"115\" r=\"2.2\" fill=\"#278A42\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#000\">\n<text x=\"45\" y=\"112\">Numerical</text><text x=\"45\" y=\"125\">substructure</text>\n<text x=\"149\" y=\"112\">Phase-lead</text><text x=\"149\" y=\"125\">compensator</text>\n<text x=\"299\" y=\"112\" class=\"it\">C(s)</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"125\">Controller</text>\n<text x=\"381\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">λ</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"119\">Adaptive</text><text x=\"381\" y=\"131\">factor</text>\n<text x=\"489\" y=\"106\" class=\"it\">H(s, k)</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"119\">Transfer system</text><text x=\"489\" y=\"131\">and specimen</text>\n<text x=\"488\" y=\"27\">NCF OSI</text>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"98\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d(t)</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">u(t)</text>\n<text x=\"225\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\">−</text>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\" font-weight=\"700\">v(t)</text>\n<text x=\"589\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">y(t)</text>\n<text x=\"394\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">d<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" class=\"it\">f<tspan dy=\"3\" font-size=\"9\">m</tspan></text>\n</g>\n<text x=\"426\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#278A42\" class=\"sm\">update</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Hybrid simulation loop with the NCF OSI framework setup.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The plant in Figure 4 is characterized by the transfer function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to the measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The open-loop transfer function and closed-loop response from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq1\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq2\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(2)</span></div>\n<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are the Laplace transforms of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The NCF stability margin of the outer controller loop is evaluated for the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> under unity feedback [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-glover\">4</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq3\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>inf</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(3)</span></div>\n<p>As the specimen changes during a hybrid simulation, the loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> changes with it. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> could be applied directly only if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> were identified with high confidence at every frequency, but online measurements rarely support this across the full band. The NCF OSI is therefore estimated from three complementary branches, direct identification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, output-power inference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and prior identification of the initial state <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which the following sections develop in turn.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s2\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.1</span>Direct identification branch, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></h2>\n<p>Identifying the closed-loop transfer function requires the autospectrum of reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of measured actuator displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is also needed to calculate coherence, which helps set the confidence weight at each frequency. A moving window lets these spectra follow changes in the specimen during the experiment.</p>\n<p>Each window contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> samples of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the sampling frequency and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the window duration. Updates occur at intervals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, so consecutive windows overlap when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The update index is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> indexes samples within each window. The Hann-windowed discrete Fourier transforms are</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq4\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(4)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq5\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munderover><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(5)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> are the samples of window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is the angular frequency of bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. The samples of each window are weighted by the Hann window presented by Blackman and Tukey [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-blackman\">5</a>]:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq6\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>cos</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(6)</span></div>\n<p>Welch [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-welch\">6</a>] proposed estimating the power spectrum by averaging, with equal weight, the squared Fourier magnitudes of overlapping windowed segments of a record. Cross spectra are estimated in the same way from products of paired Fourier transforms, with one transform conjugated. In the present study, the plant changes as the specimen degrades, so recent windows should carry more weight than earlier ones. Each window therefore provides one raw estimate of the autospectra of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> and the cross spectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq7\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(7)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq8\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(8)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq9\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(9)</span></div>\n<p>where the superscript <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the complex conjugate. These raw estimates are averaged with the geometric moving average proposed by Roberts [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-roberts\">7</a>], which updates an average from its previous value and the newest observation. Earlier spectra are retained, but their contributions decay as new windows arrive. This reduces scatter between updates while still following changes in the plant. The first complete window initializes the average. At each subsequent update, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the new spectrum and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> weights the previous average:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq10\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(10)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq11\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(11)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq12\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(12)</span></div>\n<p>The NCF OSI is evaluated over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, the set of frequencies considered in the analysis.</p>\n<p>At each selected frequency, the loop response can be identified directly from the reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and the measured response <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. The averaged spectra give the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> estimate of the closed-loop frequency response [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-bendat\">3</a>] and the magnitude-squared coherence</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq13\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(13)</span></div>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq14\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(14)</span></div>\n<p>Inverting Equation <a href=\"#eq2\">(2)</a> gives the identified loop:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq15\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(15)</span></div>\n<p>Here, the identified loop is smoothed with the running median over neighbouring frequencies discussed by Di Matteo et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-dimatteo\">8</a>], so the median is taken over each frequency and its two neighbours. The smoothed loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is hence given by</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq16\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>median</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><msub><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(16)</span></div>\n<p>The identified margin then follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq17\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(17)</span></div>\n<p>The quality of this estimate varies with time and frequency for two reasons. First, the command can be too weak for the measured response to rise above the sensor noise. Second, even an active command concentrates its energy in a limited band set by the ground motion and the structural modes, so bins outside that band carry little information.</p>\n<p>Each bin therefore receives an identification weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> between zero and one, built from three factors. The first is command activity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, detected from the root mean square of the window:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq18\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(18)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>RMS</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the root mean square of the command samples in the considered window <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mi>u</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the standard deviation of the command noise measured in a quiet record, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a hyperparameter to be tuned. The second factor is the input leverage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which reduces the weight of bins that carry little command energy. The command power in each bin is first normalized by its maximum over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq19\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BD;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(19)</span></div>\n<p>The raw leverage cannot serve as the weight directly. It is proportional to the bin power, so a bin with one tenth of the peak power would receive one tenth of the weight even though its estimate is still reliable. The weight should instead stay close to one while a bin carries enough command power and fall toward zero only when it carries too little. The hyperparameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sets this boundary:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq20\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(20)</span></div>\n<p>which stays close to one in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and decreases smoothly toward zero in bins with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0226A;</mo><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. A bin with command power equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> times the peak receives half weight. The third factor is the coherence, which completes the weight</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq21\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>&#x003B3;</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(21)</span></div>\n<p>This weight sets the share of this branch in the fusion of Section 3.1.3.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s3\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.2</span>Output-power inference branch</h2>\n<p>Direct identification fails when the command is weak or does not excite the relevant frequency band. Even then, a degrading specimen can still drive the system toward instability. However, the change in output power relative to the initial state can reveal loop amplification even at frequencies without strong excitation. This change therefore provides a second source of evidence for a reduction in the stability margin. This method is inspired by Wu and Wang [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-wu\">9</a>], who used active-power deviation and its rate of change to discriminate changes in the transient operating regime of a virtual synchronous generator. To quantify this change, the averaged output power is compared with its initial reference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq22\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(22)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the observed power ratio, which measures the change in output power at each frequency relative to the initial state. The averaged autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> from Equation <a href=\"#eq11\">(11)</a>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the reference autospectrum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> recorded in the initial state.</p>\n<p>The output-power branch detects a change by comparing the present output power with a reference from the initial state. A single window of output power fluctuates strongly, so a reference taken from one window could bias every later comparison. The reference autospectrum is therefore the median of three raw autospectra recorded in the initial state. These are taken at 0.5, 1.0 and 1.5 s, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq23\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>med</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(23)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> are the corresponding updates. The median discards one unusually loud or quiet window.</p>\n<p>According to the observation discussed in Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>] and the experimental verification presented in Section 2.4, the change due to specimen degradation can be represented by an amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> of the open-loop gain. Consequently, a candidate loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> predicts the ratio</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq24\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>g</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(24)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the predicted power ratio, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the loop of the prior identification of the initial state. The amplification is then selected as the candidate in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> that minimizes the logarithmic difference between the observed and predicted ratios:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq25\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></munder><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(25)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the selected amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> is a grid of candidate values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The predicted ratio in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a> assumes that only the loop has changed since the reference was recorded. At frequencies without excitation, however, the output consists mainly of sensor noise. Its power is estimated from few windows and therefore scatters. The reference is also the median of only three windows. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> can indicate an amplification that does not exist, and the estimated margin would decrease without degradation.</p>\n<p>The identified margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> cannot correct this error, because it is valid only at the excited frequencies. Degradation, however, changes the loop at all frequencies. At the excited frequencies, it appears as an increase in the magnitude of the identified loop. Section 2.4 observed such an increase by a factor of about 2.4. A real amplification inferred from output power should therefore be accompanied by an increase in the identified loop. The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> measures this increase and thus confirms whether the amplification indicated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is real.</p>\n<p>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the real scale that best fits the prior loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> to the identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the weighted least-squares sense:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq26\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>arg&#x2009;min</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x0211D;</mi></mrow></munder><munder><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(26)</span></div>\n<p>The factor is real, consistent with the real amplification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> in Equation <a href=\"#eq24\">(24)</a>. Each term of the sum expands as</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq27\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mo>Re</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(27)</span></div>\n<p>Setting the derivative with respect to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C1;</mi></mrow></math> to zero gives</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq28\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>Re</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>j</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(28)</span></div>\n<p>The weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> lets the well-identified excited frequencies dominate. The identified loop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is taken before frequency-median smoothing. For an unchanged specimen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, whereas for the damaged specimen of Section 2 <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The confirmation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> sets how much the amplification inferred from output power is trusted. It depends on the loop increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> identified at the excited frequencies:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq29\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(29)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the confirmation threshold. Once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals one, and the amplification inferred from output power is fully trusted. The lower bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> is a safety minimum. It accounts for amplification that cannot be observed at any excited frequency, such as amplification from unmodeled dynamics or higher-mode effects. The accepted amplification is</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq30\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(30)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the averaging equation, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a>.</p>\n<p>The accepted amplification at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inherits the scatter of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and therefore varies between updates. To reduce this scatter while following the degrading specimen, successive accepted amplifications are averaged recursively. As for the spectra in Equations (10)–(12), more weight is placed on recent values. The average is formed on a logarithmic scale:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq31\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(31)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smoothed amplification, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the averaging factor that weights the newest accepted amplification. Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> then gives the margin of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq32\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(32)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the output-power margin.</p>\n<p>To reduce false positives and chattering at small changes, the evidence from this branch is weighted by the size of the smoothed amplification up to the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq33\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(33)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the detection weight, limited to the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the amplification threshold above which the evidence is fully trusted. Consequently, the detection weight rises linearly from zero at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to one at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Together with the identification weight, it sets the weight of this branch:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq34\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(34)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the weight of the output-power branch, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the identification weight from Equation <a href=\"#eq21\">(21)</a>. Consequently, this branch receives the share left by direct identification, in proportion to the detected amplification.</p>\n<p>Unlike direct identification, which reflects both losses and gains in the stability margin, the output-power branch detects only open-loop gain amplification. Consequently, once the adaptive controller restores stability and the output power returns to its initial level, this branch cannot reflect a recovered loop. A recovery step is therefore introduced. While the median of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> remains below one, the smoothed amplification is reduced gradually:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq35\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>log</mi><msub><mover><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mrow><mi>&#x00073;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(35)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the recovery factor, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The reduction stops once the output power rises above its initial reference. As a result, the output power itself limits how far the amplification is released.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s4\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.3</span>Per-frequency fusion and reported indicator</h2>\n<p>The third branch is the margin of the prior identification of the initial state, evaluated with the current adaptive factor:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq36\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(36)</span></div>\n<p>At each frequency, the three branches now provide three margin estimates, which are combined according to the evidence behind each. The prior receives the weight left by the two measured branches:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq37\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(37)</span></div>\n<p>The three margins are then combined with these weights at each frequency bin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq38\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">w</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(38)</span></div>\n<p>Fusion is performed bin by bin, before any reduction over frequency, so margins evaluated at different frequencies are never combined. This choice also handles the idle case without special treatment. When the command is inactive, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in every bin. An unchanged loop then reads its gain-scaled prior, and a bin with a remembered change continues to report it.</p>\n<p>To drive the adaptive controller, the fused curve is reduced to a single value. The NCF OSI margin is taken as the lowest value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq39\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(39)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the NCF OSI margin at update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> is the set of frequencies considered in evaluating the NCF OSI. The adaptive law then uses it to update <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Figure 5 summarizes one complete update, from the measured signals to the adaptive factor, and Table 2 lists the estimator parameters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s5\"><span class=\"secno\">3.1.4</span>Adaptive factor update</h2>\n<p>The NCF OSI estimated in Section 3.1.3 provides the margin needed to adjust the adaptive controller factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>. When the margin falls below a specified target, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is reduced to maintain the stability of the system. This reduction is intended to counteract the gain amplification caused by specimen degradation, as discussed earlier in Section 2.</p>\n<p>To achieve this while retaining as much tracking performance as possible, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> is updated from the difference between the estimated margin and a target margin:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq40\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(40)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the margin error, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the target NCF margin. A negative error indicates insufficient margin, and a positive error indicates spare margin.</p>\n<p>Reducing and restoring the adaptive factor carry different risks. A slow reduction leaves the loop exposed while the margin is insufficient. Conversely, a fast recovery can raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> back toward instability before the estimate settles. The adaptive factor is therefore reduced and restored at different rates:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq41\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>&#x003BA;</mi><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(41)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.32</mn></mrow></math> is the ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate. Consequently, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> falls at full rate when the margin is below the target and recovers about three times more slowly. The adaptation gain is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the smallest permitted adaptive factor.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig2\"><svg role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"f2t f2d\" viewBox=\"0 0 1000 575\">\n<title id=\"f2t\">Structure of one estimator update</title>\n<desc id=\"f2d\">Windowed displacements u and y feed a windowed FFT that forms the averaged autospectra and cross spectrum. These spectra feed the direct identification and output-power branches. A third branch is the initial-state prior. The adaptive factor lambda, shown in green, feeds the output-power and initial-state prior branches. The three margin curves are fused per frequency bin with the weights w_id, w_pow and w_0, and the minimum of the fused curve over the analysis band gives b_OSI, which updates the adaptive factor lambda.</desc>\n<defs>\n<marker id=\"a2\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#000\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2l\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/></marker>\n<marker id=\"a2y\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 Z\" fill=\"#007CA8\"/></marker>\n</defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"175\" width=\"190\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"30\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"175\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"320\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"175\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n<rect x=\"700\" y=\"320\" width=\"180\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\"/>\n\n<path d=\"M2 211.7 H38\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M230 230 H280 V85 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M280 211.7 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 85 H655 V202.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 230 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M610 375 H655 V257.5 H698\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M790 285 V318\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n<path d=\"M880 375 H992\" marker-end=\"url(#a2)\"/>\n\n</g>\n<path d=\"M2 248.3 H38\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#007CA8\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#a2y)\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2A9A48\" stroke-width=\"1.8\">\n<rect x=\"340\" y=\"465\" width=\"270\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EAF7EE\"/>\n<path d=\"M940 375 V515 H612\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M340 515 H310 V248.3\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 248.3 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n<path d=\"M310 375 H338\" marker-end=\"url(#a2l)\"/>\n</g>\n<circle cx=\"280\" cy=\"211.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/><circle cx=\"940\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#000\"/>\n<circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"375\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#2A9A48\"/>\n<g text-anchor=\"middle\" dominant-baseline=\"central\" style=\"fill:#000\">\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"197\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px\">u</text>\n<text x=\"14\" y=\"234\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:22px;fill:#007CA8\">y</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Windowed FFT</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">uu</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yy</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">p̄</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">yu</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (4)–(12)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"58\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Direct identification</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"86\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L̂</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">w</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"114\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (13)–(21)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Output-power inference</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">q</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">obs</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">, </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">ĝ</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">s</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (22)–(35)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Initial-state prior</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">L</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\"> → </tspan><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan><tspan dy=\"-5\">(ω)</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (36)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"203\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Per-bin fusion</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"231\" style=\"font-size:19px\" class=\"it\">β(ω)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"259\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equations (37)–(38)</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"348\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Minimum over Ω</text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"376\" style=\"font-size:19px\"><tspan class=\"it\">b</tspan><tspan style=\"font-size:13px\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"790\" y=\"404\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (39)</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"487\" style=\"font-size:20px\">Adaptive factor</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"515\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:19px\">λ</text>\n<text x=\"475\" y=\"543\" style=\"font-size:15px;fill:#6b6b6b\">Equation (41)</text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"70\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">id</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"640\" y=\"215\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">pow</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"634\" y=\"360\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">w<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">0</tspan></text>\n<text x=\"960\" y=\"358\" class=\"it\" style=\"font-size:18px\">b<tspan style=\"font-size:12px;font-style:normal\" dy=\"5\">OSI</tspan></text>\n\n</g></svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Flowchart for calculating the NCF OSI margin <i>b</i><sub>OSI</sub>. The reference <i>u</i> and measured displacement <i>y</i> provide spectra for direct identification and output-power inference. These estimates are combined with the initial-state prior, and the resulting margin updates the adaptive factor <i>λ</i>.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"s32\"><span class=\"secno\">3.2</span>Calibration</h2>\n<p>The framework of Section 3.1 contains 16 parameters, listed in Table 2. Seven of them are design values set by the test and the analysis. The window duration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the update interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> are defined in Section 3.1.1. The window duration of 0.5 s gives a frequency resolution of 2 Hz, which also sets the lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>. The update interval of 0.125 s sets how often the indicator and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> are updated. The upper bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math>, 80 Hz, matches the band over which the plants were identified in Section 2.3. The grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math> spans the amplifications expected from degradation. The target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math> follows the guideline of Gomaa et al. [<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#ref-gomaa\">1</a>], and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> keeps part of the controller active.</p>\n<p>The remaining nine are hyperparameters. They set how quickly the indicator follows a change, how much evidence each branch requires, and how strongly the adaptive factor responds. Their values depend on the laboratory, the equipment, the specimen and its material. They therefore cannot be fixed from theory and must be calibrated against cases with a known margin.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab1\"><figcaption><b>Table 2.</b> Estimator parameters. Smoothing coefficients follow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>exp</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003C4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from the listed time constant.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Value</th><th><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C4;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Type</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.125 s</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Update interval</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5 s, 1024</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Window length at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow></math> Hz</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>2–80 Hz, 37 bins</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Analysis set, 58–62 Hz rejected</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.39</td><td>0.25 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Spectral averaging, Equations (10)–(12)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>4.54</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Activity threshold relative to command noise, Equation <a href=\"#eq18\">(18)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math></td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Input-leverage reference, Equation <a href=\"#eq20\">(20)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D4A2;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.5–6, 25 values</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Candidate loop-magnitude changes, Equation <a href=\"#eq25\">(25)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003F5;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.1</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Minimum confirmation, Equation (29)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003C1;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.5</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Confirmation threshold, Equation (29)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.12</td><td>1.0 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Output-power memory, accumulation, Equation <a href=\"#eq31\">(31)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.73</td><td>0.4 s</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Recovery factor per update, Equation <a href=\"#eq35\">(35)</a></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.40</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Target NCF margin, Equation (40)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.625</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Adaptation gain per update, Equation (41)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math></td><td>0.32</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Ratio of the recovery rate to the reduction rate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>0.20</td><td>—</td><td>Design</td><td>Smallest adaptive factor, Equation (41)</td></tr><tr><td><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></td><td>1.75</td><td>—</td><td>Calibrated</td><td>Amplification threshold, Equation <a href=\"#eq33\">(33)</a></td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>The two plants identified in Section 2 provide such cases. Because both models are known, the true NCF margin can be computed at every update and compared with the estimate. To impose damage numerically, both models receive the same command <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and their outputs are mixed:</p>\n<div class=\"equation\" id=\"eq42\"><div class=\"expression\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div><span class=\"eqno\">(42)</span></div>\n<p>where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math> are the outputs of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> is the damage index. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> represents the initial state and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> represents the damaged state. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> is constant, superposition makes this mixture equivalent to the plant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The true margin therefore follows from Equation <a href=\"#eq3\">(3)</a> applied to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>C</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The mixture interpolates the responses of the two states and does not represent an intermediate stiffness.</p>\n<p>The calibration cases combine three features. The first is the damage history. Each case lasts 120 s. Sudden damage steps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> from zero to one at 40 s and back to zero at 80 s. Gradual damage ramps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math> up between 20 and 50 s and back down between 70 and 100 s. The second feature is the excitation band. The reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is band-limited white noise with an RMS of 0.15 mm. Sensor noise with a standard deviation of 0.05 mm is added to the measured displacement.</p>\n<p>Wideband excitation covers 2 to 80 Hz and therefore includes the frequencies of the minimum margin, near 35–38 Hz. Narrowband excitation covers only 2 to 10 Hz. Direct identification then cannot observe the change at those frequencies, and only output-power inference can detect it. The third feature is the adaptive factor. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed at one, the cases test whether the indicator estimates the margin correctly. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> active, the same cases test whether the loop is held at the target while tracking is retained. Table 3 lists the resulting eight cases.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_cases\"><figcaption><b>Table 3.</b> Calibration cases.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Damage history</th><th>Excitation</th><th>Adaptive factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Wideband, 2–80 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Sudden</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gradual</td><td>Narrowband, 2–10 Hz</td><td>Active</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Figures 6 and 7 show the cases with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> fixed and active. Each column shows one case in three panels. The first shows the command and the measured displacement. The second shows the damage index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>. The third compares the estimated <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> with the true margin and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and shows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math> in the adaptive cases.</p>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_fixed\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_fixed.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 1 to 4 with the adaptive factor fixed\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Calibration cases 1–4 with the adaptive factor fixed at one.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure id=\"fig_cal_adapt\"><img src=\"figures/fig_calibration_adaptive.png\" alt=\"Calibration cases 5 to 8 with the adaptive factor active\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:100%;display:block;margin:0 auto;background:#fff\"/><figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Calibration cases 5–8 with the adaptive factor active.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Four simple metrics summarize each case after the first 5 s. The estimation error is the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The roughness is the median of the second difference of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, multiplied by 1000, so steady changes are not penalized. The travel is the total variation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math>, which measures how much the controller is adjusted. The unsafe time is the percentage of time with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow></math>. Table 4 lists the results.</p>\n<figure class=\"table\" id=\"tab_metrics\"><figcaption><b>Table 4.</b> Calibration metrics with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption><table class=\"border-white/10\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Estimation error</th><th>Roughness</th><th>Travel of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></mrow></math></th><th>Unsafe time (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.2</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.016</td><td>11.6</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>—</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.2</td><td>—</td><td>25.1</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.2</td><td>2.54</td><td>3.4</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>0.017</td><td>11.4</td><td>2.14</td><td>0.0</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>0.075</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.03</td><td>34.8</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>0.073</td><td>2.3</td><td>0.33</td><td>25.1</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>\n<p>Each case constrains particular hyperparameters. The wideband cases set the spectral averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the activity threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the leverage reference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> of direct identification. The narrowband cases set the output-power parameters. Their damage onset sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B7;</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and their return to the initial state sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The adaptive cases set the adaptation gain <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BA;</mi></mrow></math>. The values in Table 2 were tuned on a wider benchmark, which also included a closed-loop experimental record and a replay of a recorded hybrid simulation. The eight cases above illustrate their behavior.</p>\n<h1 id=\"appA\"><span class=\"secno\"></span>Appendix A. Assumptions and limitations</h1>\n<p>The estimator relies on four assumptions, and each one limits how the reported indicator should be interpreted.</p>\n<ol class=\"assumptions\"><li>The identification branch assumes that the output disturbance is uncorrelated with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>. Feedback through the numerical substructure can violate this assumption and bias <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> even at high coherence.</li><li>The output-power branch assumes that the disturbance spectrum is unchanged from the reference period and that the first 1.5 s represent the initial loop at full gain. A change in disturbance intensity or in the command-driven response can therefore alter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> without any change in stability.</li><li>The factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> is real. The output-power branch therefore captures changes in loop magnitude but not in loop phase.</li><li>Band limitation means that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is not a guaranteed lower bound on the full-frequency margin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, because frequencies outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></math> are not evaluated.</li></ol>\n<section class=\"references\" aria-labelledby=\"refs\"><h2 id=\"refs\">References</h2><ol>\n<li id=\"ref-gomaa\">Gomaa, M., Patino, E. D., Villamizar, J. N., Condori Uribe, J. W., and Dyke, S. J. (in press), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=An+online+stability+indicator+for+real-time+hybrid+simulation+emphasizing+physical+substructure+degradation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">An online stability indicator for real-time hybrid simulation emphasizing physical substructure degradation</a>,” author manuscript.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-maghareh\">Maghareh, A., Fu, Y., Montoya, H., Condori, J., Wang, Z., Dyke, S. J., and Montoya, A. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+reflective+framework+for+performance+management+%28REFORM%29+of+real-time+hybrid+simulation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A reflective framework for performance management (REFORM) of real-time hybrid simulation</a>,” <i>Frontiers in Built Environment</i>, 6, 568742. DOI: 10.3389/fbuil.2020.568742</li>\n<li id=\"ref-bendat\">Bendat, J. S., and Piersol, A. G. (2010), <i><a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Random+Data%3A+Analysis+and+Measurement+Procedures\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Random Data: Analysis and Measurement Procedures</a></i>, 4th ed., Wiley, Hoboken, NJ.</li>\n<li id=\"ref-glover\">Glover, K., and McFarlane, D. (1989), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Robust+stabilization+of+normalized+coprime+factor+plant+descriptions+with+H%E2%88%9E-bounded+uncertainty\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robust stabilization of normalized coprime factor plant descriptions with H<sub>∞</sub>-bounded uncertainty</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Automatic Control</i>, 34(8), 821–830. DOI: 10.1109/9.29424</li>\n<li id=\"ref-blackman\">Blackman, R. B., and Tukey, J. W. (1958), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+measurement+of+power+spectra+from+the+point+of+view+of+communications+engineering+%E2%80%94+Part+I\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The measurement of power spectra from the point of view of communications engineering — Part I</a>,” <i>The Bell System Technical Journal</i>, 37(1), 185–282. DOI: 10.1002/j.1538-7305.1958.tb03874.x</li>\n<li id=\"ref-welch\">Welch, P. D. (1967), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=The+use+of+fast+Fourier+transform+for+the+estimation+of+power+spectra%3A+A+method+based+on+time+averaging+over+short%2C+modified+periodograms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The use of fast Fourier transform for the estimation of power spectra: A method based on time averaging over short, modified periodograms</a>,” <i>IEEE Transactions on Audio and Electroacoustics</i>, 15(2), 70–73. DOI: 10.1109/TAU.1967.1161901</li>\n<li id=\"ref-roberts\">Roberts, S. W. (1959), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Control+chart+tests+based+on+geometric+moving+averages\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Control chart tests based on geometric moving averages</a>,” <i>Technometrics</i>, 1(3), 239–250. DOI: 10.1080/00401706.1959.10489860</li>\n<li id=\"ref-dimatteo\">Di Matteo, S., Viall, N. M., and Kepko, L. (2021), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=Power+spectral+density+background+estimate+and+signal+detection+via+the+multitaper+method\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Power spectral density background estimate and signal detection via the multitaper method</a>,” <i>Journal of Geophysical Research: Space Physics</i>, 126(2), e2020JA028748. DOI: 10.1029/2020JA028748</li>\n<li id=\"ref-wu\">Wu, H., and Wang, X. (2020), “<a href=\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar?q=A+mode-adaptive+power-angle+control+method+for+transient+stability+enhancement+of+virtual+synchronous+generators\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">A mode-adaptive power-angle control method for transient stability enhancement of virtual synchronous generators</a>,” <i>IEEE Journal of Emerging and Selected Topics in Power Electronics</i>, 8(2), 1034–1049. DOI: 10.1109/JESTPE.2020.2976791</li>\n</ol></section>\n"}];
window.MANUSCRIPT_WORD = {"eq1": "L(s,λ,k)=λ C(s) H(s,k)", "eq2": "T(s,λ,k)=(Y(s))/(U(s))=(L(s,λ,k))/(1+L(s,λ,k))", "eq3": "b_(\"NCF\") (L)=\"inf\"┬ω  (|1+L(jω)|)/√(2(1+|L(jω)|^2)).", "eq4": "U_n (ω_m)=∑_(r=0)^(N−1)▒〖w[r] u_n [r] e^(−j2πmr/N)〗,", "eq5": "Y_n (ω_m)=∑_(r=0)^(N−1)▒〖w[r] y_n [r] e^(−j2πmr/N)〗,", "eq6": "w[r]=1/2 [1−cos((2πr)/(N−1))].", "eq7": "p_(uu,n) (ω_m)=|U_n (ω_m)|^2,", "eq8": "p_(yy,n) (ω_m)=|Y_n (ω_m)|^2,", "eq9": "p_(yu,n) (ω_m)=Y_n (ω_m) U_n^* (ω_m),", "eq10": "p̄_(uu,n) (ω_m)=(1−η_s) p̄_(uu,n−1) (ω_m)+η_s p_(uu,n) (ω_m),", "eq11": "p̄_(yy,n) (ω_m)=(1−η_s) p̄_(yy,n−1) (ω_m)+η_s p_(yy,n) (ω_m),", "eq12": "p̄_(yu,n) (ω_m)=(1−η_s) p̄_(yu,n−1) (ω_m)+η_s p_(yu,n) (ω_m).", "eq13": "T̂_n (jω)=(p̄_(yu,n) (ω))/(p̄_(uu,n) (ω)),", "eq14": "γ_n^2 (ω)=(|p̄_(yu,n) (ω)|^2)/(p̄_(uu,n) (ω) p̄_(yy,n) (ω)).", "eq15": "L̂_n (jω)=(T̂_n (jω))/(1−T̂_n (jω)).", "eq16": "L̂_(\"s\",n) (jω_m)=\"median\"┬(i∈{m−1,m,m+1})  L̂_n (jω_i).", "eq17": "b_(\"id\",n)=b_(\"NCF\") (L̂_(\"s\",n)).", "eq18": "A_n={■(1,&\"RMS\"(u_n)>η_A σ_u,@0,&\"otherwise\",)┤", "eq19": "ℓ_(\"raw\",n) (ω)=(p̄_(uu,n) (ω))/(\"max\"┬(ν∈Ω)  p̄_(uu,n) (ν)).", "eq20": "ℓ_n (ω)=(ℓ_(\"raw\",n) (ω))/(ℓ_(\"raw\",n) (ω)+ℓ_(\"floor\") ),", "eq21": "w_(\"id\",n) (ω)=A_n γ_n^2 (ω) ℓ_n (ω).", "eq22": "q_(\"obs\",n) (ω)=(p̄_(yy,n) (ω))/(p_(yy,0) (ω)),", "eq23": "p_(yy,0) (ω)=\"med\"{p_(yy,n_1) (ω),p_(yy,n_2) (ω),p_(yy,n_3) (ω)},", "eq24": "q_(\"model\",n) (ω,g)=〖|(1+L_0 (jω))/(1+λ_n g L_0 (jω))|〗^2,", "eq25": "ĝ_n (ω)=\"arg min\"┬(g∈𝒢)  |log q_(\"obs\",n) (ω)−log q_(\"model\",n) (ω,g)|,", "eq26": "ρ_n=\"arg min\"┬(ρ∈ℝ) ∑_(ω∈Ω)▒〖w_(\"id\",n)^2 (ω) |L̂_n (jω)−ρ λ_n L_0 (jω)|^2〗.", "eq27": "|L̂_n−ρ λ_n L_0|^2=|L̂_n|^2−2ρ Re{λ_n L_0^* L̂_n}+ρ^2 |λ_n L_0|^2.", "eq28": "ρ_n=(Re{∑_(ω∈Ω)▒〖w_(\"id\",n)^2 (ω) λ_n L_0^* (jω) L̂_n (jω)〗})/(∑_(ω∈Ω)▒〖w_(\"id\",n)^2 (ω) |λ_n L_0 (jω)|^2〗).", "eq29": "c_n=(ρ_n−1)/(ρ_(\"th\")−1),  c_n∈[ε,1],", "eq30": "g̃_n (ω)=1+c_n (ĝ_n (ω)−1),", "eq31": "log ĝ_(\"s\",n) (ω)=(1−η_g) log ĝ_(\"s\",n−1) (ω)+η_g log g̃_n (ω),", "eq32": "b_(\"pow\",n)=b_(\"NCF\") (λ_n ĝ_(\"s\",n) L_0),", "eq33": "d_n (ω)=(ĝ_(\"s\",n) (ω)−1)/(g_(\"th\")−1),  d_n (ω)∈[0,1],", "eq34": "w_(\"pow\",n)=(1−w_(\"id\",n) ) d_n,", "eq35": "log ĝ_(\"s\",n) (ω)=r_g log ĝ_(\"s\",n−1) (ω),", "eq36": "b_(0,n)=b_(\"NCF\") (λ_n L_0).", "eq37": "w_(0,n)=(1−w_(\"id\",n) )(1−d_n ).", "eq38": "β_n (ω)=w_(\"id\",n) b_(\"id\",n) (ω)+w_(\"pow\",n) b_(\"pow\",n) (ω)+w_(0,n) b_(0,n) (ω)", "eq39": "b_(\"OSI\",n)=\"min\"┬(ω∈Ω)  β_n (ω),", "eq40": "e_n=b_(\"OSI\",n)−b_(\"tar\"),", "eq41": "λ_(n+1)={■(λ_n exp(κ e_n),&e_n<0,@λ_n exp(μ κ e_n),&e_n≥0,)┤  λ_(n+1)∈[λ_\"min\",1],", "eq42": "y(t)=[1−δ(t)] y_0 (t)+δ(t) y_d (t),"};
